␀colortbl␀green␀blue␀stylesheet␀pict␀listtable␀listoverridetable␀generator␀par␀pard␀plain␀ulnone␀cf␀qj␀sl␀Arial␀Times New Roman␀Courier New␀Lucida Console␀Consolas␀Old English Text MT␀Broadway␀Arial Black␀␀@ö#<Fö#<Vö#<Vö#<@ö#<bö#<qö#<zö#<Žö#<—ö#<J<␀␀␀␀<␀Uª␀<ªªª␀<ª␀␀␀<␀ª␀␀#<␀ªª␀(<UUU␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀´0Bô0B”ABˆ§B`ŠBSans␀Serif␀Mono␀Courier␀Slant␀Computer␀LCD␀Old English␀Block␀␀␀␀ ÷#<<(<ˆy(<Ìb(<4E(<%(<X␀(<„Õ'<´£'<%÷#<ØÚ(<ÀÊ(<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀+÷#<È˜)<°‹)<˜})<€m)<hI)<P!)<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀0÷#<÷(<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀8÷#<8)<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀>÷#<ðè(<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀G÷#< )<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀K÷#<xž(<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Žö#<®(<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀W÷#<¨¼(<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀J<␀␀␀␀<␀Uª␀<ªªª␀<ª␀␀␀<␀ª␀␀#<␀ªª␀(<UUU␀<␀␀␀People & leadership␀Leading & staying calm␀leader leadership calm stress decide meeting delegate␀People copy the calmest person in the room. Your job is to be that person, even when you don't feel it.

== Calm yourself first
 * Box breathing: in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4, for five rounds. It slows your heart and clears your head.
 * Lower your voice and slow down. A slow, low voice tells everyone's nervous system things are under control.
 * Eat, drink, sleep. A hungry, thirsty, tired leader makes bad calls. Name a second-in-command so you can rest.
 * Write it down. Getting the problem onto paper takes it out of your head and shows what to do first.

== Giving direction
 * One task, one person, one deadline: "Sam, fill every jug from the tub by noon."
 * Say why in one sentence. People work harder when they know the reason.
 * Have them repeat it back when it matters, like water treatment or fuel handling.
 * Check, then thank. Follow up at the deadline and give credit in front of others.
 * Give everyone a job. Action shrinks fear. Kids too: counting cans, carrying kindling, keeping the log.

== Making decisions
 * Ask three things: What do we know? What's the worst that could happen? What's the smallest safe step now?
 * A good decision now beats a perfect one too late. Say "we'll check again tonight."
 * For big, lasting decisions (letting people join, moving, big trades), hear from everyone, then decide clearly.
 * Write decisions and the reasons in the logbook. It ends "you never said that" arguments.

== Daily rhythm
 * Morning meeting, 10 minutes: weather, news, jobs, problems, safety reminder.
 * Evening meeting, 10 minutes: what got done, supplies count, who's sick, tomorrow's plan, something good that happened.

== Keeping people with you
 * Tell the truth, plainly. Say what you know, what you don't, and when you'll know more. Rumors fill silence.
 * Fair is everything. Same rations and same share of dirty jobs, leaders included. Post the rules and rotation.
 * Bad news: say it early, say it straight, and say what you're doing about it.
 * Settle fights early. Hear both sides privately, then decide out loud. Small grudges become big ones when people are hungry and cold.
 * Admit mistakes fast. It builds more trust than pretending.
 * Lead from the front. Do the hard jobs sometimes. People follow what you do more than what you say.␀Human nature in a crisis␀people reaction panic psychology behavior stress phases rumor hoarding normalcy␀Real disasters show people help each other far more often than they panic. The danger comes later, when people are tired, hungry and scared for a long time.

== The usual stages
 * First hours, shock: disbelief, freezing, or carrying on as if it's a normal day ("normalcy bias"). People look around to see what others are doing before they act. Clear direction snaps them out of it.
 * First days and weeks, pulling together: neighbors share and pitch in. Build your trade and trust networks now, while goodwill is high.
 * Weeks to months, wearing down: disappointment, tiredness, short tempers, blame, and old feuds coming back. Expect it. Plan routines, rest and fair rules for it.
 * Long term, rebuilding: people organize around whatever groups held together. Be one of those groups.

== Patterns to expect
 * Rumors spread fastest when information is scarce. Give regular, honest updates.
 * Hoarding and grabbing when people fear running out. Calm, visible fair sharing prevents it.
 * Blame and scapegoats. Groups under stress look for someone to blame, often outsiders or the weakest person. Stop it early.
 * Going along with the group even when it's wrong. Invite someone to argue the other side on big decisions.
 * Decision fatigue. After many choices, people make worse ones. Make routine choices into fixed rules.
 * Reputation matters more than ever. People remember who kept their word and who didn't.

== Watch for
 * Not sleeping, not eating, snapping, withdrawing, or drinking more. Give that person rest and a lighter job, and talk with them.
 * Kids act out fear as clinginess, bed-wetting, nightmares or anger. Keep routines, chores, school and play, and tell them what's happening in simple words.
 * Elders hide pain and illness. Check on them every day.
 * Desperate outsiders. Hungry people act differently than they normally would. Keep a low profile and never show your stores.␀Organizing the household or block␀organize roles jobs rotation schedule rules ration records ledger newcomers council␀== Jobs to assign (one person can hold several)
 * Coordinator: runs meetings and makes the final call.
 * Quartermaster: keeps the supply ledger and hands out rations.
 * Water & sanitation: collects and treats water, handles toilets and garbage.
 * Cook: plans meals from what's oldest first and keeps the kitchen clean.
 * Wood & fuel: cuts, stacks and logs fuel.
 * Medic: runs the first-aid kit, the meds list and daily health checks.
 * Gardener: seeds, beds, watering and seed saving.
 * Comms: radio check-ins, the news log and the message board.
 * Watch: in pairs, in short shifts at night.
 * Teacher: daily lessons for kids, skill-sharing for adults.
 * Recorder: keeps the logbook of decisions, trades and events.

== A sample day
Time | What
Sunrise | Check the watch report, fire, water, breakfast
Morning | 10-minute meeting, then the heavy work while cool and fresh: wood, water, garden, repairs
Midday | Main meal, rest, school for kids
Afternoon | Lighter work: food prep, mending, trading, teaching
Evening | 10-minute meeting, supplies count, radio check-in, lock-up
Night | Watch in 2-hour shifts, everyone else sleeps

== Rationing
 * Ration from day one. Stretching food early is easy. Running out is not fixable.
 * Days of food = total calories stored / (people x 2,000). Plan for the long version.
 * Hard workers, nursing mothers and growing teens need more. Decide this openly.
 * The cook serves and the quartermaster records. No one helps themselves to the stores.

== Records to keep
 * Supply ledger (in, out, date, who)
 * Trade log
 * Health log and meds list
 * Duty roster
 * Decisions and rules
 * Garden log: what was planted where and when, and how it did

== Rules
 * Few, written, posted, and the same for everyone.
 * Cover the basics: water and food handling, fire safety, quiet hours, watch duty, how decisions get made, what happens when rules are broken.

== Taking in new people
 * Decide the policy before someone shows up hungry at the door.
 * Ask what they can do and what they need. A skilled person (nurse, mechanic, farmer) can be worth more than the food they eat.
 * A trial period with clear jobs and rules works better than all-or-nothing.
 * When you can't take people in, point them to a shelter, church or community kitchen, and share information if you can't share food.

== Neighborhood council
 * One rep per household or street, weekly meeting at a set place and time.
 * Agenda: safety, water, sick and elderly check, trade day, shared projects (a well, a big garden, wood cutting).
 * Keep minutes and post them on the board.␀Morale & mental health␀morale mental health depression grief stress sleep hope faith routine␀Keeping spirits up is a survival skill. People who keep hope and a routine work better, get sick less and make better choices.
 * Routine: the same wake time, meals, work and bedtime every day. It makes chaos feel manageable.
 * Sleep: dark, quiet and warm. Rotate the night watch so no one goes without.
 * Daylight and movement: get outside every day, especially in a Michigan winter.
 * Small wins: mark them out loud: the first egg, a fixed bike, a full woodpile.
 * Fun on purpose: card games, music, stories, reading aloud, a weekly potluck or game night.
 * Meaning: faith, prayer, keeping a journal, or helping others. Purpose carries people through hard times.
 * Grief: loss of home, routines or people is real. Give it time and space. Holding a small memorial helps.
 * Alcohol: keep it limited. It makes people colder, sadder and more reckless, and it's poor sleep.
 * Check in: ask each person "how are you really doing?" each week, one on one.

== When it's serious
 * Signs: talking about being a burden, giving things away, hopelessness, not eating or getting up for days.
 * Ask directly and kindly. Stay with them. Remove dangerous items. Bring in someone they trust, and get professional help as soon as it can be reached.␀Kids, elders, babies & pets␀kids children family elders baby infant formula diapers pets dogs cats school␀== Kids
 * Tell them the truth in simple words: what happened, what you're doing, and that grown-ups are keeping them safe.
 * Give age-sized jobs. Ages 4-7: carry kindling, match socks, feed chickens. Ages 8-12: haul water, garden, mind little ones. Teens: real adult jobs with supervision.
 * Keep school going, short and daily (see Learn tab).
 * Safety rules: stay in sight, never touch wires or fuel cans, stay away from the stove and water, know the meeting spot, know the family code word.
 * Let them play. Play is how kids handle fear.

== Babies
 * Breastfeeding is the safest food: no water, fuel or bottles needed. Nursing mothers need extra food and water.
 * Powdered formula must be made with safe water: boiled and cooled, or bottled. Ready-to-feed formula needs no water.
 * Store several weeks of formula, plus cloth diapers, covers and pins, and a bucket for washing.
 * Babies dehydrate fast. Watch for fewer wet diapers, no tears, a sunken soft spot, or sleepiness.

== Elders and people with disabilities
 * Keep a list for each person: meds, doses, doctor, equipment, allergies.
 * Have spare glasses, hearing aid batteries, a cane or walker, and incontinence supplies.
 * Battery or manual backups for powered equipment (oxygen, CPAP, lifts). Ask the supplier now what to do in an outage.
 * Check daily for cold, dehydration and confusion. Elders feel cold and thirst less.

== Pets
 * Store a month of food plus extra water. Keep ID tags and photos.
 * Dogs are good early warning at night. Keep cats in, since they roam.
 * Pet food and vet supplies make useful trade goods.␀Water & sanitation␀Water: finding, storing, treating␀water treat boil bleach filter well rain snow storage sodis distill␀== How much
 * 1 gallon per person per day for drinking and basic washing, more in heat, for nursing mothers or with hard work. Pets need water too.
 * Goal: at least 2 weeks stored. A family of 4 for 14 days needs 56 gallons.

== Storing it
 * Use food-grade containers: water jugs, blue drums, or cleaned soda bottles. Never use milk jugs (they leak and break down) or containers that held chemicals.
 * Tap water from a treated city supply keeps well. Replace it every 6 months. Store-bought bottled water is good to its date.
 * Keep it cool and dark, off concrete, away from fuel and chemicals.
 * Write the date on every container.

== Hidden water in the house
 * Water heater: 30-50 gallons. Turn off the power or gas, open a hot faucet upstairs, then drain from the valve at the bottom. Sediment at first is normal.
 * House pipes: open the highest faucet, then catch water from the lowest one.
 * Toilet tanks (not bowls, and not if the tank has cleaner in it), ice, and liquid from canned fruit and vegetables.

== Outside water
 * Rain: catch it off a clean roof into barrels with screened lids. 1 inch of rain on 1,000 sq ft of roof is about 600 gallons. Treat it before drinking.
 * Snow: melt it, never eat it frozen, because it lowers body heat. It takes a lot of snow to make a little water. Treat it.
 * Creeks, ponds and lakes: moving, clear water is better. Collect upstream of people and animals.
 * Avoid: floodwater, water near fuel, farms or factories, anything with a fuel smell or a sheen, and pools (fine for flushing and washing, not drinking).

== Treating it (kills germs)
 * Clear it first: let it settle, then pour it through cloth, a coffee filter or a sand-and-charcoal bucket filter.
 * Boil: a rolling boil for 1 minute (3 minutes above 6,500 ft). Pour it back and forth between clean containers to improve the taste.
 * Bleach: plain unscented bleach. 8 drops per gallon for 6%, 6 drops for 8.25%. Stir and wait 30 minutes. It should smell faintly of chlorine; if not, repeat and wait 15 more minutes. Bleach loses strength after about a year.
 * Filters: a 0.1-0.2 micron gravity or squeeze filter removes bacteria and parasites. Add bleach or boil to also get viruses. Backflush often and don't let it freeze.
 * Sunlight (SODIS): clear plastic PET bottles (2 liters or smaller) of clear water, on a dark surface in full sun for 6 hours, or 2 days if cloudy. A backup method only.
 * Distilling: boil water and catch the steam on a lid that drips into a cup. It's slow but removes salt and many chemicals.

== What treatment won't fix

Fuel, pesticides, heavy metals and most chemicals. Find a different source instead.

== Wells
 * A shallow well with an electric pump needs a generator or solar to run, or a hand pump. Ask a well company about a hand pump that fits next to the electric one.
 * Shock-chlorinate a well that's been flooded before using it.␀Toilets, hygiene & laundry␀sanitation toilet bucket latrine hygiene handwash soap laundry dishes garbage flies rats menstrual bathing␀More people get sick from poor sanitation after a disaster than from almost anything else. Make this a top job, not an afterthought.

== Toilets
 * If the sewer still drains: flush by pouring a bucket of gray water fast into the bowl.
 * Bucket toilet: a 5-gallon bucket lined with a heavy bag. A toilet seat or a pool noodle slit on the rim. Cover each use with sawdust, peat, dry leaves, wood ash or kitty litter.
 * Two-bucket system: pee in one, poop in the other. Pee is mostly sterile and can be diluted 1:10 on non-food plants or poured away from water. Dry poop smells far less.
 * Getting rid of waste: tie off bags and bury them 6-8 inches deep and at least 200 ft from wells, streams and gardens, or keep them in a covered pit until pickup returns.
 * Latrine for a group: a trench 1 ft wide, 2-3 ft deep, downhill and 200 ft from water. Cover each use with soil, and fill it in when it's 1 ft from the top.

== Hand washing (most important)
 * A jug with a spigot (or a "tippy tap": a jug on a string, tipped with a foot pedal), soap and a catch bucket at the toilet and the kitchen.
 * Wash for 20 seconds: before cooking and eating, after the toilet, after diapers and after caring for the sick.
 * Hand sanitizer helps when there's no water, but not on dirty hands.

== Dishes
 * Three tubs: hot soapy wash, clean rinse, then sanitize with 1 tablespoon unscented bleach per gallon for a minute. Air dry.
 * Scrape food scraps to chickens or compost first.

== Bathing
 * A sponge bath with a basin of warm water each day. Face, armpits, groin and feet matter most.
 * Keep feet dry and change socks daily. Wet feet cause sores and infection.
 * Cut nails short to keep germs out.

== Laundry
 * A clean plunger in a bucket makes a washing machine. Wash, rinse twice, wring, and hang to dry indoors in winter.
 * Wash underwear, socks and bedding of the sick separately, in the hottest water you can.
 * Wool can go many days between washes. Air it out.

== Garbage & pests
 * Sort it: food scraps to compost or animals, burnables to the stove (no plastics), cans flattened and stored, the rest bagged in lidded bins.
 * Flies spread disease. Cover food and toilets, and keep scraps sealed.
 * Rats follow garbage and stored food. Use metal bins and snap traps, and keep the stores off the floor.
 * Mosquitoes: dump standing water weekly.

== Women's needs
 * Store pads and tampons, or switch now to washable cloth pads or a menstrual cup. They last for years.
 * Soak cloth pads in cold water, then wash with soap and dry in sun if possible.␀Food storage & shelf life␀food storage shelf life rice beans rotate pests mylar oxygen absorbers buckets calories year supply vitamins␀Store what you eat, eat what you store, and use the oldest first. Keep it cool, dry, dark and sealed. Heat is the enemy: every 10-15 degF cooler can roughly double shelf life.
Food | Sealed, cool & dry
White rice, wheat berries, dry corn, rolled oats, dry pasta | Up to 25-30 years in mylar or #10 cans with oxygen absorbers. About 1-2 years in store packaging.
Dried beans, lentils, split peas | 10+ years sealed. Old beans take longer to soften; pressure cooking helps.
Salt, sugar, honey, pure maple syrup (unopened) | Indefinite. Warm crystallized honey gently.
Canned vegetables, meat, beans, soups | 2-5 years. Toss bulging, leaking, rusted-through or spurting cans.
Canned tomatoes, fruit, juice | 12-18 months (the acid wears on the can)
Powdered milk | Many years sealed and cool. Whole milk powder goes rancid sooner.
Peanut butter, cooking oil, shortening | About 1-2 years. They go rancid, which you'll smell.
White flour | About 1 year. Store wheat berries and grind them instead.
Brown rice, whole wheat flour, nuts | Only months (their oils spoil). Freeze if you can.
Freeze-dried meals | Up to 25 years sealed
Home-canned food | Best within 1 year, safe longer if the seal holds

== Packing for the long haul
 * Use mylar bags inside food-grade buckets with oxygen absorbers (follow the package for the size), sealed with a hot iron.
 * Only dry foods (under 10% moisture) go with oxygen absorbers. Sugar and salt go without them, or they turn to a brick.
 * Label each bucket with the contents, date packed and calories.
 * Gamma-seal lids on the buckets you're eating from make them easy to open.

== Keeping pests out
 * Rodents chew through bags. Use buckets, glass jars or metal cans.
 * Freeze grain for 3-4 days before storing to kill insect eggs.
 * Keep stores off the floor and away from walls, and check them monthly.
 * Bay leaves in flour and rice help keep weevils away.

== How much (a common one-year guideline per adult)
 * About 300-400 lb of grains (wheat, rice, oats, corn, pasta)
 * About 60 lb of beans and lentils
 * About 60 lb of sugar or honey
 * About 10 quarts of oil or fat
 * About 8 lb of salt, 16 lb of powdered milk, plus vitamins, spices, baking soda, yeast

Start with 2 weeks, then 3 months, then work up. Calories: about 2,000-2,500 a day per adult, and 3,000+ for hard work in the cold. A pound of dry rice or beans is about 1,600 calories.

== Don't forget
 * Vitamin C: scurvy can start after 1-3 months without it. Store vitamins, and grow sprouts, cabbage, kale, potatoes and peppers. Rose hips and berries help too.
 * Fat: a diet of lean meat and grains alone leaves people weak and hungry. Store oil, peanut butter and nuts, and render lard.
 * Sprouting: beans, lentils, wheat and alfalfa sprout in 2-5 days in a jar. They give fresh vitamins in winter.
 * Comfort foods (coffee, tea, cocoa, candy, spices) boost morale and are top barter goods.
 * A manual can opener, or two.␀Preserving food␀canning dry smoke salt cure root cellar preserve botulism pressure ferment jerky eggs water glass freeze␀== Canning
 * Water-bath canning is only for high-acid foods: fruit, jam, jelly, pickles and tomatoes with added lemon juice or citric acid.
 * Pressure canning is a must for low-acid foods: vegetables, beans, meat, fish, soups and stock. Water-bath canning these can cause botulism, which can kill.
 * Follow a tested recipe (USDA guide, Ball Blue Book, or a state extension office) exactly. Don't change the jar size, thickness or acid.
 * Most of Michigan is under 1,000 ft. Higher places need more time or pressure; the recipe says how much.
 * Lids: standard flat lids are single-use for canning. Reusable lids (like Tattler) and spare flats make good trade goods.
 * Check seals after 24 hours. The center shouldn't flex. Store without the rings, so a failed seal shows.
 * Throw out any jar that's leaking, spurting, bulging, moldy or bad-smelling. Don't taste it.
 * A pressure canner works on a wood stove or camp stove if you can hold steady heat.

== Drying
 * Slice 1/4 inch thick. Dry in a solar dryer (a screened box with a dark bottom and vents), on screens near the wood stove, or in a barely warm oven.
 * Fruit is done when leathery, vegetables when crisp. Store in sealed jars and check for moisture after a week.
 * Jerky: lean meat in thin strips, fat trimmed. Heat the meat to 160 degF (165 degF for poultry) before or after drying to kill germs. It keeps 1-2 months at room temperature, longer cold.
 * Herbs: hang in bunches somewhere dark and airy.

== Fermenting
 * Sauerkraut: shredded cabbage plus salt at about 2% of its weight (about 11/2 tsp per pound). Pack it hard in a crock until the juice covers it, and weigh it down. 3-6 weeks at 60-70 degF.
 * Pickles and other vegetables work the same way in brine. Scum on top can be skimmed. Fuzzy colored mold or a rotten smell means throw it out.
 * It needs no fuel or jars, adds vitamin C, and is good for digestion.

== Salting, curing & smoking
 * Salt-curing draws water out of meat and fish. It needs a lot of salt and cold temperatures, under 40 degF. Learn it from someone experienced or a tested recipe.
 * Smoking adds flavor and dries the surface. Hot smoking cooks the meat; cold smoking alone doesn't make meat safe.
 * Rendering lard and tallow keeps fat for months, and fat sealed over cooked meat (confit) helps it keep.

== Cold storage
 * Root cellar: 32-40 degF, dark and humid, for potatoes, carrots, beets, turnips, cabbage and apples. A basement corner, an insulated room, or a buried trash can or cooler with straw works.
 * Winter squash, pumpkins, onions and garlic like it drier and around 50 degF. Keep apples away from potatoes, because apples make them sprout.
 * Carrots and beets keep in damp sand. Potatoes need total dark (light makes them green, and green potatoes are toxic).
 * Michigan winter: below-freezing weather is a free freezer. Use a sealed cooler kept in shade and safe from animals. Watch out for thaws.

== Eggs
 * Fresh, unwashed eggs keep several weeks cool. Store them pointy end down.
 * Water glassing: clean, unwashed, uncracked eggs in a solution of about 1 oz pickling lime per quart of water, in a covered crock kept cool. They keep many months. Rinse before using.␀Cooking without power␀cooking cook stove rocket haybox retained heat solar oven bread fuel saving food safety␀== Cooking setups
 * Camp stove (propane or white gas): outdoors or with strong ventilation only.
 * Rocket stove: burns a few handfuls of sticks very hot and clean. You can build one from bricks or #10 cans in an hour.
 * Wood stove top: cooks and heats at once. A Dutch oven inside the firebox on coals bakes bread.
 * Grill or fire pit: outdoors only. Cast iron over coals, not flames.
 * Solar oven: a box with a black pot, foil sides and a glass or plastic window reaches about 200-300 degF on sunny days. Good for beans, rice, stew and bread. It uses no fuel.

== Save fuel
 * Haybox (retained-heat) cooking: bring the pot to a boil for a few minutes, then pack it tight in a box or cooler surrounded by blankets, pillows or straw. It keeps cooking for hours on no fuel. Great for rice, beans, oats and stew. Reheat to a boil before serving.
 * Soak beans overnight, or 24 hours, and grains overnight.
 * A pressure cooker cuts cooking time to a third or less.
 * Always put a lid on. Cut food small. Cook one big pot once a day.
 * Heat wash water on the stove after cooking, while the fire is still hot.

== Simple staples
 * Flatbread: flour, water, a pinch of salt and some fat, rolled thin and cooked in a dry hot skillet. No oven or yeast needed.
 * Rice and beans: together they make a complete protein. Add any vegetable, fat and spice.
 * Oatmeal: a fast hot breakfast. Add sugar, fat and dried fruit for calories.
 * Cornbread: cornmeal, flour, powdered milk and baking soda, baked in cast iron.
 * Soup: stretches anything. Bones, scraps and any vegetables, simmered.
 * Sourdough starter: flour and water left to sour. It raises bread forever without store-bought yeast. Feed it daily.

== Food safety without a fridge
 * Bacteria grow fast between 40 degF and 140 degF. Cooked food left out more than 2 hours (1 hour in heat) should be eaten hot or thrown away.
 * Cook only what you'll eat at that meal. Bring leftovers to a full boil before eating.
 * Cook meat thoroughly: 165 degF for poultry and wild game, 160 degF for ground meat, 145 degF for whole cuts and fish.
 * When in doubt, throw it out. Food poisoning without a doctor is dangerous.␀Gardening & what to plant (Michigan)␀garden plant seeds grow crops calendar frost potatoes beans squash corn michigan soil compost seed saving pests yield␀Southeast Michigan is roughly hardiness zone 6. The last frost is around early to mid May and the first frost around early to mid October. That gives about 150 frost-free days. Check your exact dates with the MSU Extension.

== Planting calendar
When | Plant
February-March (indoors, sunny window) | Start onions, peppers, then tomatoes and cabbage family about 6-8 weeks before last frost
Late March-April (soil workable, not muddy) | Peas, spinach, lettuce, radish, onion sets, kale, turnips
Mid April-mid May | Potatoes, carrots, beets, parsnips, chard, cabbage and broccoli transplants
After last frost (mid-late May) | Beans, corn, squash, pumpkins, cucumbers, tomato and pepper transplants, sunflowers
June | More beans and corn every 2-3 weeks for a steady harvest
July-early August | Fall crops: carrots, beets, kale, turnips, lettuce, spinach, peas
October | Garlic, planted pointy end up, mulched, harvested next July

== Most food for the work
 * Potatoes: more calories per square foot than almost anything you can grow here. Plant cut pieces with 2 eyes each, 12 inches apart, and hill up soil as they grow. In a good year you get about 10 lb for each pound planted.
 * Dry beans (pinto, black, kidney): protein that stores for years. Let the pods dry on the plant.
 * Winter squash and pumpkins: keep 3-6 months with no canning.
 * Corn: flint or dent corn dries for cornmeal. Plant in blocks of at least 4 rows, not single rows, so it pollinates.
 * Three Sisters: corn, pole beans and squash together. The corn holds up the beans, the beans feed the soil, and the squash shades out weeds.
 * Kale, collards, cabbage: survive frost and keep producing into winter.
 * Sunflowers: oil-rich seeds for people and chickens.
 * Feeding one person mostly from a garden takes thousands of square feet and years of practice. Start now and treat the garden as a big supplement to stored food.

== Soil
 * Pick the sunniest spot, with 6-8 hours of direct sun.
 * No-dig beds: lay cardboard over grass, then pile on 6-12 inches of compost, leaves, grass clippings and manure. Plant right into it.
 * Compost: mix "greens" (food scraps, grass, manure) with "browns" (leaves, straw, cardboard) about 1:2, keep it damp like a wrung-out sponge, and turn it now and then.
 * Wood ash adds potash and raises pH. Use a light dusting, not on potatoes (it causes scab).
 * Chicken and rabbit manure are excellent. Compost chicken manure first, because fresh it burns plants.

== Water, weeds & pests
 * About 1 inch of water a week. Water deep and less often, early in the day, at the soil not the leaves.
 * Mulch with straw or leaves 3-4 inches deep. It holds water and stops weeds.
 * Hand-pick beetles and caterpillars into soapy water. Row covers keep bugs off cabbage family plants.
 * Fences: deer need 8 ft, or two shorter fences 3 ft apart. Rabbits need chicken wire buried 6 inches deep.
 * Rotate crop families each year to cut down disease.

== Seed saving
 * Buy open-pollinated or heirloom seeds. Their seeds grow true next year; hybrid (F1) seeds don't.
 * Easiest to save: beans, peas, lettuce and tomatoes, which self-pollinate.
 * Beans and peas: let pods dry brown on the plant, then shell.
 * Tomatoes: scoop the seeds into a jar with a little water and let it ferment 3 days. Rinse, then dry on a plate.
 * Squash crosses with other squash of the same kind, so grow one variety of each kind, or hand-pollinate and tape the flowers shut.
 * Corn crosses easily over long distances. Save from a large number of plants to keep it strong.
 * Store seeds cool, dry and dark in labeled envelopes inside a jar. Most keep 2-5 years. Onion and parsnip seed last only about a year.
 * Seeds are among the best barter goods.␀Foraging, fishing & hunting␀forage wild food plants berries nuts mushrooms fishing fish hunting deer game trapping license dnr␀Wild food helps, but it's seasonal and takes skill. Follow Michigan DNR laws, licenses and seasons unless officials say otherwise. Rules protect the game you'll need next year too.

== Wild plants (only if you're 100% sure)
 * Common, easy to recognize: dandelion (leaves, flowers, roots), plantain, lamb's quarters, purslane, chickweed, wild onion and ramps, blackberries and raspberries, mulberries, elderberries (cooked only), wild grapes, black walnuts, hickory nuts, acorns (leach out the bitterness in water first), cattail shoots and roots.
 * Deadly look-alikes exist: poison hemlock and water hemlock look like wild carrot (Queen Anne's lace). Many berries are poisonous. Learn plants in person from a local expert and a regional field guide.
 * Mushrooms: don't eat wild mushrooms unless an experienced local forager confirms each one. Some deadly ones look like good ones.
 * Maple sap in late winter: tap sugar maples when days are above freezing and nights below. About 40 gallons of sap boil down to 1 gallon of syrup.

== Fishing
 * Michigan is full of lakes. Panfish (bluegill, perch, crappie) are easy on simple gear: small hooks, line, bobbers, split shot, and worms or crickets.
 * Ice fishing extends the season. Ice must be 4+ inches of clear, solid ice to walk on. Stay off ice that's slushy, dark, near moving water or near structures.
 * Clean fish quickly and keep them cold. Cook to 145 degF.
 * Check Michigan's "Eat Safe Fish" guide for which fish from which waters are safe to eat often. Some carry mercury or PCBs.

== Hunting & trapping
 * Take a hunter safety course. It's required in Michigan for most new hunters.
 * Deer, rabbits, squirrels and waterfowl are common. Small game is easier and more reliable than big game.
 * Field-dress large game right away and cool the meat fast. Hang it at 34-40 degF if you can.
 * Cook wild game to 165 degF. Wear gloves when cleaning. Don't eat animals that looked sick.
 * Trapping needs a license and knowledge of the rules. Done well, it's efficient food.
 * A deer gives very roughly 40-60 lb of usable meat. Preserve it by canning, drying or freezing in winter.
 * Rabbit is very lean. Eat it with fat or other foods. Lean meat alone isn't enough to live on.␀Small livestock␀chickens eggs rabbits goats ducks bees livestock animals coop feed predators␀Check your local ordinances, which often limit roosters and animals in town. Start before you need it, because animals take learning.

== Chickens (best first animal)
 * A good laying hen gives about 4-6 eggs a week when young, fewer in winter and as she ages.
 * Space: about 4 sq ft per bird inside the coop, 8-10 sq ft in the run. Dry, draft-free, with ventilation up high.
 * Feed: about 1/4 lb a day each, plus kitchen scraps, garden weeds and bugs. Grit and oyster shell for strong eggshells.
 * Water: they need it unfrozen daily in winter. Rotate pans or use a heated base when you have power.
 * Predators (raccoons, foxes, hawks, dogs): lock them in at dusk, use hardware cloth instead of chicken wire, and bury it or apron it out 12 inches.
 * Cold-hardy breeds for Michigan: Buff Orpington, Plymouth Rock, Australorp, Wyandotte, Rhode Island Red.
 * A broody hen hatches her own chicks if you have a rooster.
 * Bonus: their manure fertilizes the garden and they eat ticks and bugs.

== Rabbits
 * Quiet, small, and they breed fast. Pregnancy is about a month, with litters of around 6-10.
 * They eat hay, grass, garden scraps and pellets, and need shade and protection from heat.
 * Their manure can go straight on the garden without composting.
 * The meat is very lean, so pair it with fat in the diet.

== Others
 * Ducks: hardier than chickens in wet cold, and their eggs are rich. They make a mess with water.
 * Goats: milk and meat. They need strong fencing, company (never keep just one), and real knowledge.
 * Bees: honey (which keeps forever), wax for candles, and pollination. Learn from a local beekeeping club.␀Shelter, heat & fuel␀Staying warm␀warm heat cold winter insulate layers clothing sleep shelter windows tent hypothermia␀== Shrink the space you heat
 * Pick one room, ideally on the south side, small, with few windows and near the stove. Everyone lives and sleeps there.
 * Close the other rooms off. Hang blankets over doorways and stuff towels along the bottoms of doors.
 * Cover windows with blankets, bubble wrap, foam board or plastic sheeting. Open south curtains on sunny days, and close everything at sunset.
 * Set up a tent or a blanket fort inside the room for sleeping. It traps body heat.
 * Rugs, cardboard or foam on the floor. Cold floors pull heat out of you fast.

== Dress for it
 * Wear layers: a base layer of wool or synthetic, a fleece or wool middle layer, and a wind- and water-resistant outer layer.
 * Avoid cotton when it's cold and wet. Wet cotton pulls heat away and won't dry.
 * Wear a hat, scarf, gloves and wool socks. Keep a dry set of socks for sleeping.
 * Stay dry. Sweat chills you, so take off a layer before working hard.
 * Loose is warmer than tight. Tight boots and too many socks cut off circulation.

== Sleeping warm
 * Insulate underneath you first (pads, folded blankets, cardboard). More heat goes down than up.
 * Sleep together. Share body heat.
 * Eat a fatty snack before bed and use the bathroom first. A full bladder makes you colder.
 * Hot water bottle: a sealed Nalgene or metal bottle of hot water wrapped in a sock, inside the bag at the feet or chest.
 * A warm hat while sleeping. Breathe out of the bag, not into it, so it doesn't get damp.

== Body fuel
 * Eat more in the cold: fats and proteins burn slowly and keep you warm.
 * Drink warm fluids. Dehydration makes you colder. Limit alcohol, which makes you lose heat.
 * Move: do light work or exercise to warm up, then rest before you sweat.

== Protect the house
 * Keep pipes from freezing (see the quick card) or drain them.
 * Clear snow off vents, the furnace intake and the generator exhaust.
 * Knock heavy snow off a flat or weak roof with a roof rake from the ground.
 * Seal drafts with caulk, weatherstripping or rolled towels.␀Fuel: storing & stretching␀fuel gas gasoline diesel propane kerosene storage stabilizer siphon btu heater cans␀Fuel | How long it keeps
Gasoline (with ethanol) | About 3 months, or 1-2 years with stabilizer added when fresh. Ethanol-free keeps longer.
Diesel | About 6-12 months, longer with stabilizer and biocide. Keep water out and fill tanks full.
Propane | Indefinitely in a sound tank
Kerosene (K-1) | Several years sealed, in a blue kerosene can, out of sunlight
White gas (camp fuel) | Several years sealed
Charcoal | Indefinitely if dry. Never burn it indoors.
Firewood | Years if dry, stacked off the ground and covered on top

== Storing safely
 * Use approved cans: red for gas, yellow for diesel, blue for kerosene. Never use milk jugs or glass.
 * Keep them in a detached shed or well away from the house, never in living space or the basement, and away from sparks, pilot lights and sun.
 * Don't fill to the brim. Leave room for expansion.
 * Write the date on every can and use and refill on a rotation.
 * Check local fire code limits on how much you can store. They exist for good reason.
 * Propane tanks stay outside, upright, valves closed, and never in a basement (propane sinks and collects).

== How long fuel lasts
 * A 20-lb propane tank holds about 430,000 BTU. That's roughly 2 days nonstop on a 9,000 BTU indoor-rated heater on high, longer on low.
 * A kerosene heater around 10,000 BTU burns very roughly a gallon every 12-14 hours.
 * A typical portable generator uses very roughly 1/2-1 gallon an hour depending on size and load. Run it in short blocks.
 * A car idling uses very roughly 1/4-1/2 gallon an hour.

== Safety
 * Use only heaters rated for indoor use with a low-oxygen shutoff, crack a window, and keep a CO alarm next to them.
 * Refuel heaters and generators outside, when they're cool.
 * Use only the right fuel. Gas in a kerosene heater can cause a fire or explosion.
 * Never siphon by mouth. Gas in the lungs can kill. Use a hand pump siphon.
 * Keep fuel out of reach of kids, and teach them never to play near it.

== Stretching fuel
 * Heat people, not rooms (see Staying warm). Cook once a day, with a haybox. Combine trips. Bikes before cars. Save gas for the generator, chainsaw and emergencies.␀Firewood & wood stoves␀wood firewood cord season split stack chainsaw axe stove chimney creosote kindling fire starting␀== Amount
 * A cord is a stack 4 ft high, 4 ft deep and 8 ft long. A "face cord" is often just one row, about a third of that.
 * Heating a Michigan house all winter with wood can take several cords. Heating one room takes much less.

== Seasoning (drying)
 * Fresh-cut wood is about half water. It burns cold and smoky and coats the chimney with creosote.
 * Split it and stack it in single rows, bark up, off the ground on pallets, with the top covered and the sides open to wind and sun.
 * Most hardwood dries in 6-12 months. Oak takes 1-2 years.
 * Dry wood: lighter, has cracked ends and gray color, and sounds hollow when knocked together. A cheap moisture meter should read under 20%.
 * Already dead, standing trees with no bark (not rotten) and downed limbs are often close to dry.

== Best woods
 * Most heat: oak, hickory, sugar maple, ash, beech, black locust, apple.
 * Medium: soft maple, birch, cherry, elm (hard to split).
 * Fast and light: pine, spruce, poplar, willow. Good for kindling and quick fires.
 * Never burn: painted, stained or pressure-treated wood, plywood and particleboard, trash or plastics.

== Cutting
 * Gather downed limbs and storm wood first. Felling trees is dangerous; learn from someone experienced, with proper chaps, helmet, eye and ear protection.
 * Always work with a partner. Keep a first-aid kit with a tourniquet nearby.
 * A bow saw and splitting maul work with no fuel. Split rounds along existing cracks, toward the outer edge.
 * Keep blades sharp. Dull tools slip and cause injuries.

== Stove use
 * Use only a stove or fireplace made for it, with a chimney in good shape.
 * Keep anything that can burn about 3 ft from the stove, unless the stove's label allows less.
 * Top-down fire: big logs on the bottom, smaller on top, kindling and tinder at the very top. Light it, and it burns down cleanly with little smoke.
 * Burn hot fires. A smoldering fire makes creosote, which causes chimney fires.
 * Check the chimney monthly and clean it as needed, with a brush and rods.
 * Put ashes in a metal can with a lid, outside, on the ground. Coals stay hot for days.
 * Keep a fire extinguisher and a bucket of sand near the stove. CO alarm in the room.

== Starting fires
 * Store several ways to light: lighters, strike-anywhere matches in a waterproof jar, a ferro rod.
 * Easy fire starters: cotton balls with petroleum jelly, dryer lint, birch bark (lights even when damp), pine pitch, wax-soaked cardboard.
 * Build it in this order: tinder, then pencil-thin kindling, then thumb-thick sticks, then wrist-thick wood.␀Power, light & tools␀Electricity & solar␀electricity power solar battery generator inverter pedal transfer switch watts watt-hours charge controller lifepo4 wiring␀== Power the small, important things

Lights, radio, phones, fans, medical devices, a little refrigeration. Don't try to run the whole house.

== Know the numbers

Watts are power right now. Watt-hours (Wh) are power over time: a 10 W light for 5 hours = 50 Wh.
Device | Rough use
LED bulb | 5-10 W
Phone charge | 10-20 Wh per full charge
Radio | 1-5 W
Laptop | 40-60 W while running
Box fan | 50-100 W
CPAP | 30-60 W (much more with the heated humidifier)
Refrigerator | 1-2 kWh a day, with a big surge to start
Chest freezer | About 1 kWh a day
Furnace blower | 300-800 W (gas furnaces need power to run)
Microwave, hair dryer, space heater | 1,000-1,500 W. Too much for small systems.

== Solar
 * A 100 W panel makes very roughly 300-500 Wh on a sunny Michigan summer day, and maybe a third of that in winter (short days, low sun, snow).
 * Aim it south, tilted about your latitude (around 42 deg here) for year-round, steeper in winter. Keep snow and shade off it.
 * A basic system: panel -> charge controller -> battery -> fuse -> inverter (for AC) or 12 V outlets (for DC).
 * MPPT controllers get more from the panel than PWM. A pure sine inverter is safest for electronics.
 * Solar garden lights: set them out by day, bring them inside at night as free lamps.

== Batteries
 * LiFePO4 (lithium iron phosphate): safest lithium type, lasts thousands of cycles, can use most of its capacity. Best choice, and used in most power stations.
 * Lead-acid (car, deep-cycle): cheaper and heavier. Use only about half the capacity or it wears out fast. Gives off gas while charging, so charge in a ventilated spot. Car starting batteries die quickly when drained deep.
 * Keep batteries above freezing when charging. Lithium must not be charged below freezing.
 * Put a fuse close to the battery positive. A shorted battery can start a fire.
 * Use thick enough wire for the current. Thin wire gets hot. Look up a 12 V wire gauge chart.
 * Store spare AA and AAA rechargeables (NiMH) and a USB or solar charger.

== Generators
 * Outside, 20+ ft from windows, doors and vents, with exhaust pointed away. Never in a garage, even with the door open.
 * Run in blocks: fridge 1 hour, freezer 1 hour, charge batteries, then off. A full freezer holds about 2 days closed.
 * Use heavy outdoor extension cords sized for the load.
 * Never backfeed the house by plugging into an outlet ("suicide cord"). It can electrocute lineworkers and you. Use a transfer switch or interlock installed by an electrician.
 * Change the oil on schedule and run it monthly when there's no outage. Lock it down; generators get stolen.

== Other sources
 * Pedal power: roughly 50-100 W of steady pedaling for a fit adult. Good for charging batteries in shifts.
 * Car: a running car with an inverter charges phones and batteries. Do it outdoors only, in short sessions.
 * Hand-crank radios and lights: small but endless.␀Lighting␀light lighting lamp lantern candle headlamp oil kerosene flashlight␀* Headlamps: the most useful light, since your hands stay free. One per person.
 * LED lanterns: light a room for many hours on a few batteries. Hang one from the ceiling.
 * Solar garden lights: free light each night.
 * Oil and kerosene lamps: bright and long-lasting, but a fire risk. Keep them on a stable surface, away from curtains, and never unattended. Use only the right fuel.
 * Candles: the biggest fire risk. Use them in jars or on plates, away from anything that burns, and never leave them unattended or near sleeping kids.
 * Glow sticks for kids, nightlights and emergencies.
 * Work with daylight. Get up with the sun and do the bright work early, to use less light at night.
 * Light discipline: thick curtains at night so you're not the only bright house on the street.␀Tools, repair & getting around␀tools repair fix bike bicycle small engine sewing carpentry tarp roof duct tape transportation car navigation map compass␀== Core tools
 * Hand tools: hammer, screwdrivers, pliers, adjustable wrench, socket set, hand saw, hacksaw, utility knife, tape measure, level, crowbar, shovel, rake, hoe, axe, splitting maul, bow saw, files and a sharpening stone.
 * Fasteners and fixers: nails, screws, wire, zip ties, duct tape, epoxy, rope and cord, tarps, plastic sheeting, plywood.
 * Take care of tools: clean, dry, oiled and sharp. Keep them in one place and sign them out.

== Common fixes
 * Roof leak: stretch a tarp over the ridge and nail it down with wood strips (not just nails through the tarp). Get water out fast to stop mold.
 * Broken window: plywood or plastic sheeting stapled over the frame from inside, with duct-tape edges.
 * Bike flat: pull the tube, find the hole (pump it up and listen, or dunk it in water), scuff, glue and patch. Check the tire for the thorn. Keep spare tubes and a patch kit.
 * Small engine won't start (mower, generator, chainsaw): check for fresh fuel, then spark (a clean, gapped plug), then air (a clean filter). Stale gas is the most common cause. Carb cleaner helps.
 * Clothes: sew buttons, patch holes, and darn socks with a needle, thread, safety pins and fabric scraps. Sorted (in your suite) covers bigger repairs.

== Getting around
 * Bikes are the best grid-down transportation: quiet, cheap and no fuel. Keep tubes, a pump, a chain tool and oil. A trailer carries big loads.
 * Cars: keep the tank at least half full in normal times. Save fuel for emergencies. Store a spare tire, jumper cables, a tow strap and basic tools.
 * Walking: good boots and wool socks. Plan routes and travel in pairs in daylight.
 * Paper maps of your county and state with a compass. Phones and GPS may not work. Mark water, shelters, hospitals, routes and friends' homes.
 * Compass basics: hold it flat, turn yourself until the needle sits on N, then pick a landmark in the direction you want and walk to it, again and again.
 * Without a compass, the sun rises in the east, sets in the west, and at midday sits to the south here.␀Health & medical␀First aid basics␀first aid wound bleeding shock splint fracture sprain cpr burns eye head injury cuts stitches␀Take a real class now: Red Cross First Aid/CPR and Stop the Bleed. Practice beats reading. Where There Is No Doctor (a free book from Hesperian) is worth printing.

== First look at an injured person
 * Is the scene safe for you? Don't become a second victim.
 * Big bleeding? Stop it first with pressure, packing or a tourniquet.
 * Breathing? Open the airway by tilting the head back and lifting the chin. If not breathing, start CPR.
 * Shock? Pale, cold, sweaty skin, fast weak pulse, confusion. Lay them flat, raise the legs if there's no leg or spine injury, keep them warm, and give nothing by mouth if they're badly hurt.
 * Head to toe: check for other injuries.

== Wounds
 * Clean them well: flush with lots of clean (boiled or treated) water under pressure, using a squeeze bottle or a bag with a pinhole. This matters more than any ointment.
 * Pull out dirt and splinters. Cover with a clean dressing and change it daily or when wet.
 * Close small, clean, fresh cuts with butterfly strips or wound-closure strips. Leave dirty wounds, bites and punctures open to drain, packed with clean gauze.
 * Infection signs: spreading redness, heat, swelling, pus, red streaks up the limb, fever. Red streaks or fever mean serious infection, so get skilled help.
 * Tetanus: know everyone's vaccine dates. Boosters are recommended every 10 years, or 5 for dirty wounds.

== Bones, sprains and joints
 * Fracture: splint it as it lies, padding it and tying above and below the joint, then check circulation. Sling an arm.
 * Sprain: rest, ice or cold, wrap and raise. Walk on it gently as pain allows after a couple of days.
 * Spine injury (fell from height, hit the head hard, numbness): don't move them unless they're in danger. Keep the head in line with the body.

== Head injury

Watch for 24 hours: worsening headache, repeated vomiting, confusion, unequal pupils, very sleepy or hard to wake, or a seizure. Those are emergencies.

== Eyes

Flush with clean water for 15+ minutes for chemicals or grit. Don't rub. Cover an injured eye loosely. Something stuck in the eye: don't pull it out, cover both eyes and get help.

== Burns

Cool water for 20 minutes, cover loosely, give lots of fluids. Watch for infection. Big, deep, or face, hand or groin burns are serious.

== Kit contents
 * Gloves, gauze and rolls, trauma dressings, a tourniquet, tape, butterfly strips, elastic wraps, a SAM splint, scissors, tweezers, a thermometer, a squeeze bottle, saline, antibiotic ointment, burn gel, eye wash, a blood pressure cuff, and a flashlight.␀Illness & disease␀sick illness disease fever flu diarrhea dehydration infection contagious isolation lyme mosquito food poisoning teeth dental␀== Stopping the spread
 * Clean water, clean hands and clean toilets prevent most outbreaks.
 * Sick people get their own corner, cup, dishes and towel. One caregiver, with a mask and hand washing.
 * Cough into elbows. Masks help in crowds and when caring for the sick.
 * Keep vaccines up to date now, while doctors are open.

== Fever
 * Fluids, rest, and light clothing. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen as labeled.
 * Serious signs: fever over 103 degF that won't come down, a stiff neck, a rash that doesn't fade when pressed, trouble breathing, confusion, or any fever in a baby under 3 months.

== Diarrhea and dehydration
 * Rehydration drink: 1 liter clean water, 6 level tsp sugar, 1/2 level tsp salt. Small sips often. Keep eating simple food (rice, bananas, crackers, soup) when possible.
 * Dehydration signs: dark urine or none, dry mouth, dizziness, no tears, sunken eyes. In babies: few wet diapers, a sunken soft spot, very sleepy.
 * Blood in stool, high fever, or diarrhea lasting days needs skilled care.

== Breathing illness

Rest, fluids, steam from a bowl of hot water. Danger signs: fast or labored breathing, blue lips, chest pain, confusion.

== Ticks and mosquitoes (Michigan)
 * Check everyone for ticks daily, especially hairline, ears, armpits, waist and groin. Remove them with tweezers.
 * A bull's-eye or spreading rash, fever and aches up to a month later can be Lyme disease.
 * Long pants tucked into socks and repellent help. Dump standing water to cut mosquitoes.

== Teeth
 * Brush twice a day and floss. A tooth abscess without a dentist can become very serious.
 * Clove oil eases toothache briefly. Salt-water rinses help gum sores. Swelling in the face or jaw with fever needs skilled help.

== Food poisoning

Prevent it with the 2-hour rule and thorough cooking. Treat with fluids and rest. Throw out suspect food and bulging cans without tasting them.␀Medicines & health supplies␀medicine meds prescription otc pharmacy expiration insulin storage antibiotics list supplies glasses␀== Prescriptions
 * Ask your doctor and pharmacist now about a 90-day supply and what to do in an emergency. Many states allow emergency refills during declared disasters.
 * Keep a written list for each person: medicine, dose, times, what it's for, doctor, pharmacy and allergies.
 * Rotate: use the oldest first.

== Over-the-counter stock
 * Pain and fever: acetaminophen, ibuprofen, aspirin
 * Allergy: an antihistamine like diphenhydramine or loratadine
 * Stomach: loperamide (diarrhea), antacids, oral rehydration salts, a laxative
 * Skin: antibiotic ointment, hydrocortisone cream, antifungal cream, burn gel
 * Other: cough drops, saline nasal spray, eye drops, electrolyte packets, a multivitamin, sunscreen, lip balm, lice treatment
 * Kids' versions with the right doses for their weight.

== Expiration dates
 * Many solid pills kept cool and dry keep much of their strength for some time past the date.
 * Liquids, insulin, epinephrine, nitroglycerin, eye drops and some antibiotics don't. Ask a pharmacist about your specific medicines.
 * Keep medicines cool, dry and dark, not in the bathroom or a hot car.

== Medicines that need cold
 * Many insulins keep at room temperature for about a month once out of the fridge (it varies by brand; check the insert). Keep them out of heat and never let them freeze.
 * Evaporative cooler: a small clay pot inside a bigger one, with wet sand between and a wet cloth on top, stays well below air temperature in dry weather.
 * In winter, an insulated cooler in an unheated room may be cool enough. Use a thermometer.

== Other health supplies
 * Spare glasses and your prescription, contact lens supplies, hearing aid batteries, dentures supplies, a cane, crutches, incontinence supplies, a thermometer, a blood pressure cuff, a glucose meter and strips.

Don't use antibiotics without medical guidance. The wrong one or the wrong dose can do harm and breed resistant germs.␀Safety & security␀Fire safety␀fire safety extinguisher smoke alarm candle stove ash chimney escape plan␀* House fires go up sharply during outages because of candles, heaters, stoves and generators.
 * Smoke alarm and CO alarm on every level and outside sleeping areas, with battery backups. Test them weekly.
 * ABC fire extinguishers in the kitchen, near the stove and in the garage. Everyone learns PASS: Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze, Sweep.
 * Buckets of sand or water by the stove.
 * Clear 3 ft around stoves and heaters. Never dry clothes on or right next to them.
 * Candles and lamps never unattended, never near curtains, bedding or kids.
 * Ashes in a metal can with a lid, outside, away from the house.
 * Chimney checked monthly. Signs of a chimney fire: roaring sound, dense smoke, sparks from the top. Close the stove's air intake, get everyone out, and wet the roof if it's safe.
 * Escape plan: two ways out of each room, a meeting spot outside, a practice drill each month. Ladders for upstairs windows.
 * Grease fire: lid on, heat off. Never water.
 * With no fire department, prevention is everything.␀Home & neighborhood security␀security crime looting low profile lock door watch neighbors deescalate conflict scam␀The goal is to stay out of trouble: be hard to notice, hard to target, and part of a neighborhood that looks out for each other.

== Low profile
 * Don't advertise your supplies, generator, solar, garden or animals. Talk about "getting by," not "we're set."
 * Blackout curtains at night. Generator in a shed or behind a fence, run by day if possible. Watch out for cooking smells.
 * Throw out empty packaging out of sight, not on the curb.
 * Trade in daylight, in a public spot, with a partner. Bring only what you're trading.

== Harden the house
 * Solid exterior doors with deadbolts. Use 3-inch screws in the strike plates and hinges so they reach the frame.
 * A bar or brace for doors, and pins or dowels in sliding doors.
 * Thorny bushes under windows, a dog, motion-solar lights and gravel paths all give warning.
 * Board up broken or ground-floor windows if needed.
 * Keep documents, cash and small valuables hidden in more than one place.

== Neighborhood watch
 * Watch in pairs, in short shifts, with a radio, a flashlight and a notebook. Watching and reporting is the job, not confronting.
 * Agree on signals (a whistle, horn or radio code) for "come help," "danger" and "all clear."
 * Know who lives on your street. Strangers stand out.
 * Check on the elderly and people living alone daily.

== Calming a tense situation
 * Stay calm, keep your voice low, hands visible and open, and keep space between you.
 * Listen and repeat back what they want. Don't argue, insult or corner them.
 * Offer something small that costs little: information, water, directions to help.
 * Leave an exit for them and for you. Walking away is a win.
 * Nothing you own is worth a life.

== Scams and cons

Fake officials, fake trades, "pay me now, deliver later" deals. Check ID through someone you trust, and trade face to face.␀Unrest, war & emergencies␀war unrest riot curfew nuclear fallout shelter chemical evacuate go-bag rumor propaganda air raid siren blackout␀This is civil defense: protecting your family and staying alive, not fighting.

== Information
 * Listen to official broadcasts: NOAA weather radio, AM news, emergency alerts.
 * In conflict or unrest, expect rumors and propaganda from every side. Write down where each report came from. Act on two separate trusted sources, and get ready on one.
 * Follow lawful orders and curfews. Being out after curfew draws trouble.

== Unrest nearby
 * Avoid crowds, protests, lines and checkpoints. If you get caught in one, move to the edges and away, and don't stop to watch.
 * Keep the car fueled and pointed out of the driveway. Have go-bags by the door.
 * Stay home and stay quiet if the trouble isn't heading your way. Leave early if it is.

== Go-bag (one per person)
 * Water (2 liters plus a filter), 3 days of food, meds and copies of prescriptions
 * Copies of IDs, deeds, insurance and contacts, sealed in a bag. Cash in small bills.
 * Clothing layers, rain gear, sturdy shoes, a hat, gloves
 * Headlamp, radio, batteries, phone charger, power bank
 * First-aid kit, hygiene kit, masks, toilet paper
 * Map with routes marked, compass, notebook, pen, whistle
 * Knife, lighter, cord, duct tape, an emergency blanket, a tarp
 * Kids: a comfort item and a card with names and contacts

== Evacuating
 * Plan three routes to a place to stay (family or friends in another area).
 * Decide the trigger ahead of time. Leave early. Late is when roads jam.
 * Leave a note saying where you went and when, in a place your family knows to look.
 * Shut off water. Shut off gas only if told to or if there's a leak.

== Sheltering in place
 * Nuclear warning or blast: if you see a flash, get down and cover your head, away from windows. Then get inside, stay inside, stay tuned. Go to the basement or the middle of a big building, the more walls and earth around you the better. Stay at least 24 hours unless officials say otherwise. Fallout weakens quickly over the first day.
 * If you were outside: remove your outer clothing and bag it, then wash your skin and hair with soap (no conditioner). Wipe off pets.
 * Take potassium iodide only when public health officials say to. It protects only the thyroid.
 * Chemical release: get inside, shut windows, doors and vents, turn off fans and HVAC, go to an upstairs inside room (many chemicals sink), and seal gaps with plastic and tape.
 * Biological or pandemic: stay home, masks, hand washing, isolate the sick, limit contact with outsiders.

== Blackouts and air-raid conditions
 * Cover windows completely at night. Know your siren signals and the nearest public shelter. Keep shoes, light and the go-bag by the bed.␀Weather & natural disasters␀weather storm tornado blizzard ice storm flood heat wave thunderstorm lightning michigan barometer forecast␀== Michigan's big ones
 * Blizzards and ice storms: the most likely cause of long outages here. Stay in, stay warm, and avoid travel. Ice-laden trees and lines fall for days afterward.
 * Tornadoes: most common late spring and summer. Basement, or the lowest inside room. Green sky, a roaring sound like a train, or large hail are warning signs.
 * Severe thunderstorms and lightning: "when thunder roars, go indoors." Stay in 30 minutes after the last thunder. Stay off ridges, lone trees and water.
 * Floods: spring melt and heavy rain. Move things up, stay out of moving water, and treat floodwater as contaminated.
 * Heat waves: harder without AC. Shade, water, wet cloths, cool basements, and the hottest hours spent resting. Check on elders.
 * Extreme cold: wind chill below about -20 degF can freeze skin in under 30 minutes. Cover all skin.

== Reading the weather without a forecast
 * A barometer that's falling fast means a storm is coming. Rising means clearing. A cheap analog barometer is worth having.
 * Clouds thickening and lowering from the west or southwest usually means rain within a day.
 * Tall, towering clouds building in the afternoon mean thunderstorms.
 * A ring around the moon (high thin clouds) often means rain or snow within a day or two.
 * Wind shifting to the northwest after a storm usually brings colder, clearer weather.
 * Keep a weather log. Patterns help you plan gardening and work.␀Trade, comms & learning␀Barter & trade␀barter trade money hours value ledger credit market skills goods fair␀== How value works without money
 * Hours of work are a fair yardstick. An hour of anyone's honest labor is worth an hour, and goods are priced by the work to make, find or replace them.
 * Value swings with need. Salt in a deep winter, or seeds in spring, can be worth far more than in normal times.
 * Skills keep their value and can't be stolen. A nurse, mechanic, gardener or teacher always has something to trade.

== Best trade goods
 * Consumables people run out of: salt, sugar, coffee, tea, cooking oil, spices, yeast, canning lids, batteries, lighters and matches, soap, toilet paper, toothpaste, feminine supplies, diapers, OTC medicines, seeds.
 * Small, durable goods: needles and thread, work gloves, socks, tarps, rope, nails and screws, duct tape, fishing hooks and line.
 * Food: eggs, honey, garden produce, bread, preserved goods.
 * Services: repairs, sewing, haircuts, babysitting, teaching, wood cutting, medical help, radio messages, hauling.

== Don't trade away

Your last water treatment, prescription meds, warm clothing, or anything your family needs to survive the season. Trade from surplus.

== Trading well
 * Trade face to face, in daylight, in a public spot, with a partner. Bring only what you're trading. Don't show your stores.
 * Agree on quality and amount before handing anything over. Weigh, count and inspect.
 * Keep promises small and on time. Your reputation is your currency.
 * Write every deal down: date, who, what was given and received.
 * If a deal feels pushy or too good to be true, walk away.

== Community credit ledger
 * A trusted person keeps a public book of hours: Ann fixed Bob's roof for 4 hours, so Ann is +4 and Bob is -4. Anyone can spend credit with anyone.
 * Set limits on how far below zero anyone can go. Review it at the weekly meeting.
 * This works better than one-for-one swaps, because people don't need to want each other's stuff at the same moment.

== Market day
 * Same place, same day, same time each week. Simple rules: no selling spoiled food, fair weights, disputes go to an agreed referee. Post a board of wants and offers.␀Radios & communication␀radio comms communication frs gmrs ham cb noaa frequency channel license message signal␀== Radios
Type | Good for | License
AM/FM/NOAA weather radio | News and official alerts (NOAA is on 162.400-162.550 MHz) | None, receive only
FRS walkie-talkies | House and next-door, often under a mile in town | None
GMRS | Neighborhood and town, more with repeaters | FCC license, no test, covers the family
CB | Few miles. Channel 9 is emergency, 19 is truckers. | None
Ham (amateur) | Town to town, state, world | FCC license with a test (Technician is easy to start)
Shortwave receiver | News from far away when local stations are down | None, receive only
 * Ham: 146.520 MHz FM is the national 2-meter calling channel. Local clubs and ARES groups run emergency nets. Join one now.
 * Spare batteries and a solar or hand-crank way to charge. Radios are useless dead.
 * Range depends on height. Get up high, or put the antenna higher.

== Radio habits
 * Set check-in times (like 8 am and 7 pm, 5 minutes) on an agreed channel. Saves batteries.
 * Keep it short. Think before you key the mic. Say who you're calling, then who you are.
 * Assume anyone can listen. Don't announce supplies, plans or that a house is empty.
 * Agree on simple code words for "all is well," "need help," and "danger."

== Message format

Who is it for, who is it from, what happened, where, when, and what's needed. Write it down before sending. Keep a log of messages in and out.

== No radio
 * Message board at a corner or the mailbox cluster: notes with name, date and a short message.
 * Runners: teens on bikes carrying written notes between households.
 * Signals: three of anything (whistle blasts, fires, flashes) means distress. A whistle carries much farther than a voice.
 * Family plan: meeting spot, backup spot, out-of-area contact, code word (see the Comms tab).␀Teaching, skills & books␀teaching school education learn skills books library curriculum reading math kids adults␀== Keep school going
 * Short and daily beats long and rare. Aim for 1-2 hours in the morning.
 * Reading: read aloud every day. Kids read seed packets, recipes, manuals and stories.
 * Writing: the family logbook, letters to relatives, trade records.
 * Math: measuring water and fuel, rations, garden spacing, trades, recipes doubled or halved.
 * Science: gardening, weather, water treatment, how the solar panel works, first aid.
 * History and civics: how communities have rebuilt before, how to run a fair meeting.
 * Mix ages: older kids teach younger ones, and both learn more.

== Adult skill-sharing
 * Each person teaches one skill a week. Nurses teach first aid, mechanics teach engines, gardeners teach seed saving.
 * Write down what you learn so the skill isn't lost if someone leaves.

== Skills worth learning now

First aid and CPR, water treatment, fire and wood stove care, gardening and seed saving, canning and drying, cooking from scratch, small engine and bike repair, sewing and mending, basic carpentry and plumbing, solar and battery wiring, radio, fishing, raising chickens, teaching and record-keeping, conflict resolution.

== Books worth keeping on paper
 * Where There Is No Doctor and Where There Is No Dentist (free from Hesperian)
 * USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning (free) or the Ball Blue Book
 * The Encyclopedia of Country Living (Carla Emery)
 * A regional wild plant field guide and a Michigan garden guide from MSU Extension
 * A basic car repair manual for your vehicles
 * The ARRL Ham Radio License Manual
 * Kids' books, textbooks, a dictionary, an atlas
 * Print this Rugged guide too (use the Print button in your My Apps copy)␀Supplies␀What to obtain (priority order)␀obtain get buy supplies checklist list priority gear tools stockpile␀Build in layers: 2 weeks, then 3 months, then a year. Get the top tiers first.

== Tier 1: Life-safety
 * Prescription meds (90-day supply), a good first-aid kit, tourniquets, OTC medicines, ORS
 * Water: 2 weeks stored, a gravity filter, unscented bleach, a rain barrel
 * Battery CO alarms, smoke alarms, ABC fire extinguishers
 * Warmth: wool blankets, sleeping bags rated to 0 degF, hats, gloves, wool socks, base layers
 * Headlamps, lanterns, rechargeable batteries and a solar charger
 * A hand-crank NOAA radio

== Tier 2: Food & daily living
 * 2 weeks, then 3 months of food you eat: rice, beans, oats, pasta, canned meat and vegetables, oil, peanut butter, salt, sugar, honey, powdered milk, vitamins, coffee and tea
 * A manual can opener, a camp stove and fuel, a rocket stove, cast iron, matches, lighters, a ferro rod
 * Sanitation: buckets with lids, heavy trash bags, sawdust or kitty litter, soap, bleach, toilet paper, hand sanitizer, feminine supplies, diapers
 * Plastic sheeting, tarps, duct tape, a staple gun

== Tier 3: Power, tools & comms
 * A power station or LiFePO4 battery, a solar panel, a charge controller, an inverter
 * A generator with stored, stabilized fuel and a transfer switch (by an electrician)
 * Hand tools, a bow saw, an axe and maul, shovel, rope, nails and screws, a sewing kit
 * FRS/GMRS radios, paper maps, a compass, notebooks and pens
 * Bikes, a bike repair kit, spare tubes, a trailer

== Tier 4: Long-term
 * Open-pollinated seeds, garden tools, hose, row covers
 * Canning jars, lids, a pressure canner, a dehydrator or solar dryer
 * Bulk grains and beans in mylar and buckets, a grain mill
 * Seasoned firewood (a year ahead), a wood stove if you can install one safely
 * Chickens and a coop, fishing gear
 * Barter stock: salt, coffee, lighters, batteries, soap, needles and thread, canning lids, work gloves, seeds
 * Paper books (see Teaching, skills & books)

== Documents (copies in a waterproof bag)

IDs, birth certificates, deeds, titles, insurance, medical records and meds lists, vaccination records, bank info, family photos and a contact list.␀Starting & building fires␀fire start starting tinder kindling ferro rod flint steel char cloth bow drill feather stick fatwood wet weather fire lay teepee log cabin lean-to star extinguish␀Fire needs three things: fuel, heat and air. Most failed fires have too little small fuel, wood that's too wet, or too little air.

== Gather everything before you strike

Collect two big double handfuls of each size before you light anything. Fires die while people run off for more sticks.
 * Tinder: catches a spark or flame. Dry, fluffy and fine.
 * Kindling: matchstick to pencil thick.
 * Small fuel: thumb to wrist thick.
 * Main fuel: wrist thick and up, split if possible.

== Good tinder
 * Natural: birch bark (oily, lights even damp), shredded cedar or juniper bark, dry grass, cattail fluff, pine needles, old bird or mouse nests, punky dry rotten wood, and fatwood (resin-soaked pine stumps and knots that smell like turpentine and light even when wet).
 * Homemade: cotton balls with petroleum jelly (each burns 2-3 minutes), dryer lint, wax-soaked cardboard strips or egg cartons, paper, and char cloth.
 * Char cloth: put 100% cotton scraps in a small tin with a nail hole in the lid and set it in a fire until smoke stops coming from the hole. Let it cool closed. The black cloth catches the smallest spark.
 * Feather sticks: shave thin curls down a dry stick, leaving them attached. Even in rain, the inside of a split stick is dry.

== Ways to light
 * Lighters and matches: store lots, kept dry. Strike-anywhere matches in a jar or pill bottle with the striker strip. A lighter that's too cold won't spark, so keep it in an inside pocket.
 * Ferro rod: hold the striker still against the tinder and pull the rod back, so the sparks land on the tinder without scattering it. It works wet and for thousands of strikes.
 * Flint and steel: strike the steel down across a sharp flint edge, sending sparks onto char cloth held against the flint. Fold the glowing cloth into a tinder bundle and blow gently until it flames.
 * Magnifying glass: focus sunlight to a tiny bright dot on char cloth or dark tinder.
 * Battery and steel wool: touch both terminals of a 9-volt battery to fine (0000) steel wool. It glows instantly. Keep steel wool and batteries stored apart.
 * Bow drill: a friction method using a bow, spindle and fireboard. It's hard to learn, so practice in good times before you depend on it.

== Fire lays (how to stack it)
 * Teepee: kindling leaned in a cone over the tinder. Lights fast and burns hot. Good for starting and boiling water.
 * Log cabin: sticks stacked in a square around a small teepee. Burns evenly into a solid bed of coals. Best for cooking.
 * Lean-to: kindling leaned against a bigger log that blocks the wind.
 * Star: a few big logs pointing into the center like spokes. Push them in as they burn. Uses little wood for a long, low fire.
 * Top-down (upside-down): big wood on the bottom, smaller on top, tinder at the very top. Burns down slowly with little smoke and little tending. Good in stoves.

== Wet weather
 * Look for standing dead branches (they snap with a crack), the underside of logs, and dead lower branches of evergreens.
 * Split wood to reach the dry inside. Make feather sticks.
 * Build on a platform of sticks or bark, not on wet ground or snow.
 * Carry a little dry tinder and fire starters in a sealed bag at all times.

== Cooking fires
 * Cook over coals, not flames. Flames burn the outside and leave the inside raw, and they blacken pots.
 * Hardwood gives the best coals. Rake them into a hot side and a warm side.
 * A hand held just above the cooking spot: 2 seconds means hot, 4-5 seconds medium, 6-8 seconds low.

== Safety and putting it out
 * Pick a spot clear of overhanging branches, dry grass and buildings. Clear 10 ft around and ring it with rocks (dry ones only, see Fire-resistant materials).
 * Keep water, sand or a shovel at hand. Never leave a fire alone.
 * Never use gasoline to start or boost a fire. Its fumes explode.
 * Putting it out: drown it, stir the ashes, drown again, and feel with the back of your hand. If it's too hot to touch, it's too hot to leave.␀Improvised cooking devices␀rocket stove hobo stove can stove brick stove dakota fire hole reflector oven bean hole dutch oven keyhole alcohol stove buddy burner solar oven haybox improvised cooking␀Every device here is for outdoors or a well-ventilated open space like an open garage doorway with the door fully up. None go inside a closed room.

== Rocket stove (best all-around)
 * A short, insulated L-shaped tunnel. Sticks go in the side, burn very hot, and the flame shoots up the chimney into the pot. It uses a few handfuls of sticks per meal with little smoke.
 * 16-brick rocket stove (no mortar): lay bricks in a square layer with one gap for the feed opening. Stack 3-4 more layers into a box chimney. Set the pot on three small stones or a grill on top so air can escape.
 * Can version: a #10 can for the body, a smaller can for the burn chamber and chimney, joined to a short feed tube. Fill the space between the cans with an insulator: wood ash, perlite, vermiculite or sand. The insulation makes it burn hot and clean.
 * Feed sticks in slowly from the side and push them in as they burn. A little shelf in the feed tube lets air flow under the sticks.

== Hobo stove
 * A #10 can or a small steel bucket upside down. Cut a door at the bottom to feed sticks and punch holes around the top edge for smoke. The top becomes your burner. Good for small pans and boiling water.

== Small fuel stoves
 * Alcohol can stove: made from two soda-can bottoms. It burns denatured alcohol or methanol (yellow-bottle fuel line antifreeze). The flame is nearly invisible in daylight, so be careful. Methanol is poisonous; use it outdoors and keep it away from kids.
 * Buddy burner: corrugated cardboard rolled tight in a tuna can and soaked in melted wax. Lit, it cooks slowly under a hobo stove for an hour or more. Smother it with the lid to put it out.

== Using a fire pit
 * Keyhole fire: a fire ring shaped like a keyhole. Burn wood in the round part and rake coals into the narrow slot to cook over, with a grate on two rocks.
 * Dakota fire hole: a 1-ft-deep hole for the fire with a second slanted hole beside it for air. Burns hot with little smoke and is protected from wind.
 * Tripod: three poles lashed at the top with a chain or wire to hang a pot. Raise or lower it for heat.

== Baking and slow cooking
 * Dutch oven: cast iron with a flat lid. Set it on coals and pile coals on the lid. For baking, put about twice as many coals on top as underneath.
 * Reflector oven: a foil-lined box or sheet metal folded into a sideways V that faces the fire. Food on a shelf in the middle bakes from reflected heat.
 * Bean hole: dig a hole, burn a hardwood fire in it for an hour or two to make deep coals, set in a lidded Dutch oven of soaked beans, cover with coals and then soil. Dig it up 6-8 hours later.
 * Haybox (retained heat): boil the pot for a few minutes, then bury it in a box of blankets, pillows or straw. It keeps cooking for hours with no fuel.
 * Solar oven: a box with a black pot, foil sides and a clear window. Reaches about 200-300 degF on sunny days.
 * Clay or cob oven: a dome of clay, sand and straw over a sand form on a stone base. Fire it inside, rake out the coals, then bake bread in the stored heat. A longer project, but it lasts years.

== Indoor cooking without power
 * Only on a wood stove that's vented through a real chimney, or a fireplace with a working flue.
 * Camp stoves, grills, hibachis and charcoal: outdoors only. Charcoal gives off deadly carbon monoxide with no smoke to warn you.␀Improvised heating & thermal mass␀heating heat improvised barrel drum stove fireplace thermal mass bricks stones hot water bottle candle heater flowerpot myth rocket mass heater warm bed␀The rule: anything that burns inside the house must vent its smoke outside through a proper chimney or flue. There's no safe "unvented" wood, charcoal, gas grill or camp-stove heater. Always run a battery CO alarm.

== Safest choices, in order
 * Heat people instead of the house: one small insulated room, layers, sleeping bags, body heat (see Staying warm).
 * A real wood stove or fireplace insert with a sound chimney.
 * An indoor-rated propane or kerosene heater with a low-oxygen shutoff, a window cracked, and a CO alarm.
 * Stored heat: hot water bottles, warm bricks and stones.

== Fireplaces
 * An open fireplace sends most of its heat up the chimney and can pull warm air out of the house. Close off the room around it, or use glass doors or an insert.
 * Burn small, hot fires of dry hardwood. Close the damper only when the fire is completely out and cold.
 * Put a metal or brick heat reflector at the back of the firebox to throw more heat into the room.

== Barrel or drum stoves
 * Kits turn a steel drum into a wood stove. The drum must be clean steel that never held chemicals or fuel.
 * Never heat galvanized metal (dull gray, crystal-pattern zinc coating). The fumes cause metal fume fever and can be dangerous.
 * Burn it outside once, hot, to burn off paint and residue before using it indoors.
 * It needs everything a real stove needs: a non-combustible base and wall shield, proper clearance, and a real chimney (see Chimneys & stovepipe).
 * Thin steel burns through and glows red when overfired. Line the bottom with sand or firebrick and keep fires moderate.

== Thermal mass: storing heat
 * Bricks, stones, water and earth soak up heat and release it slowly. A stove surrounded by a little brick or stone wall keeps the room warm for hours after the fire dies.
 * Jugs of water near the stove, or in a sunny south window, store heat too.
 * Warm bed stones: heat dry bricks or stones near the stove (not in the fire), wrap them in thick towels, and put them in the bed. Test them on your arm first.
 * Never put rocks from rivers, lakes or wet ground in a fire. Water trapped inside can turn to steam and make them burst.
 * Hot water bottles: a sealed metal or heavy plastic bottle (Nalgene type) of hot, not boiling, water in a sock. Check the lid twice. A burst bottle scalds.
 * Rocket mass heater: a rocket stove whose exhaust runs through a long bench of cob or brick, storing heat for a day. Very efficient, but a real build project with real fire risk. Learn from detailed plans and an experienced builder.

== Myths to skip
 * Candle or tealight flowerpot heaters: a candle gives off about the heat of an old lightbulb. A flowerpot doesn't add heat; it only hides the flame, and it's a fire risk. Use candles for light only.
 * Oven or gas range for heat: a gas oven left on can make deadly CO.
 * A grill in the garage with the door open: CO still builds up and seeps into the house.␀Chimneys & stovepipe␀chimney stovepipe flue pipe improvised vent clearance thimble window wall roof creosote draft smoke back 3-2-10␀A stove is only as safe as its chimney. Most stove fires and CO problems come from bad pipe and too little clearance. Use a listed, factory-made chimney installed by the stove maker's instructions whenever you can. The guidance below is for emergencies.

== Clearance (the space around a hot pipe)
 * Single-wall stovepipe: keep it at least 18 inches from anything that can burn: walls, ceilings, curtains, furniture, wood trim.
 * Stoves: often 36 inches to walls without shields. Follow the stove's label if it has one.
 * Heat shields: sheet metal or cement board mounted on non-combustible spacers with a 1-inch air gap behind it (open at the top and bottom) can cut the needed clearance a lot. Follow the stove maker's or NFPA 211 clearance tables.
 * Floor: set the stove on a non-combustible hearth pad (brick, stone, tile on cement board) that extends past the front where coals might fall.

== Connecting pipe
 * Use stove pipe made for wood stoves (black steel, 24-gauge or thicker). Not dryer vent, not flexible aluminum, not galvanized pipe.
 * Put the crimped (narrow) end pointing down toward the stove, so creosote drips back inside instead of leaking out the joints.
 * Fasten each joint with 3 sheet-metal screws.
 * Run the pipe upward the whole way, at least 1/4 inch rise per foot of sideways run. Use no more than two 90 deg bends. Keep the horizontal parts short.
 * Keep the total pipe run short and straight. Long, cold, bendy pipe drafts poorly and builds creosote.

== Going through a wall or window (emergency setups)
 * Never run single-wall pipe straight through a wall, ceiling, roof or window frame. It will set the wood on fire.
 * Use a listed wall thimble or pass-through and insulated double- or triple-wall chimney pipe (Class A) wherever the pipe goes through anything.
 * Emergency window setups replace the glass with a non-combustible panel (sheet metal or cement board) with the insulated pipe through its center, well away from the wooden frame. This isn't to code; treat it as temporary, watch it closely, and check that the frame stays cool to the touch.
 * Outside, run the pipe up the wall on brackets, with a cap and spark screen on top.

== Height and draft
 * 3-2-10 rule: the chimney top should be at least 3 ft above where it leaves the roof, and 2 ft higher than anything within 10 ft. A short chimney smokes and back-drafts.
 * Warm chimneys draft better. To start a cold stove, first burn a twist of newspaper at the bottom of the flue to warm it.
 * Smoke coming back into the room: the flue is cold, the damper or air intake is closed, the cap is blocked by snow, ice or a nest, or the house is too airtight (crack a window near the stove).

== Care
 * Burn only dry wood, and burn hot. Smoldering fires coat the pipe with creosote.
 * Check the pipe and chimney monthly during heavy use. More than about 18 inch of crusty black buildup means it's time to clean it with a brush and rods.
 * Check the cap for snow, ice, nests and blockage.
 * Watch for rust-through, loose joints and black stains at the seams. Replace bad pipe sections.
 * Keep a fire extinguisher and a CO alarm in the room. See the "Chimney fire" emergency card.␀Insulation: why it matters␀insulation insulate r-value air leaks drafts windows heat loss straw leaves snow bubble wrap blankets cardboard foundation banking␀Insulation is the cheapest heat you'll ever have. Every bit of heat you keep in is wood you don't have to cut and fuel you don't have to burn. Without it, even a good stove struggles.

== How heat leaves
 * Air leaks: drafts around doors, windows, outlets, attic hatches and pipes. Often the biggest loss, and the easiest to fix.
 * Roof and ceiling: heat rises, so a poorly insulated attic loses a lot.
 * Windows: a single pane loses heat far faster than an insulated wall.
 * Walls and floors: cold floors pull heat from your feet and body.

== What makes insulation work

Trapped, still air. Fluffy materials, layers and air pockets slow heat down. Packing insulation flat or letting it get wet ruins it. R-value is the measure: the higher, the better.

== Quick wins
 * Seal drafts first: rolled towels at door bottoms, tape or caulk around windows, rope caulk, foam strips, plastic over outlets on outside walls. Feel for drafts with a wet hand, or watch a candle flame flicker.
 * Windows: plastic window film (with an air gap), bubble wrap pressed onto wet glass, foam board, or a heavy blanket or quilt hung close over the window. Open south windows to sun by day, cover everything at night.
 * Doors: hang a blanket over the inside of the door and over open doorways to unheated rooms.
 * Floors: rugs, cardboard, foam pads or blankets, especially where you sit and sleep.
 * Ceilings: in a one-room setup, a tarp or blankets hung as a low ceiling keeps warm air near you.
 * Tent indoors: a small tent or blanket fort inside the warm room traps body heat for sleeping.

== Outside the walls
 * Bank the foundation: pile snow, straw bales, or bags of leaves against the base of outside walls. It stops wind from getting under the house.
 * Snow is a good insulator when piled thick, which is why snow shelters stay near freezing inside even in deep cold.
 * Straw bales make warm temporary walls for coops, sheds and root cellars. Keep them dry and away from flame.

== Insulation everywhere else
 * Yourself: layers of wool and down are personal insulation.
 * Cooking: a haybox is insulation for a pot. An insulated rocket stove burns hotter and cleaner.
 * Food and water: insulated root cellars keep food from freezing. Wrapped pipes and tanks resist freezing.
 * Animals: a draft-free, dry coop with deep bedding keeps chickens laying.

== Safety
 * Never block a chimney, stove air intake, furnace vent or the clearance around a stove or pipe with insulation, blankets or curtains.
 * Keep paper, cardboard, plastic, foam board, straw and blankets away from heaters and stovepipes. See Fire-resistant materials.
 * A sealed-up house still needs fresh air when anything burns. Crack a window near the stove or heater and keep the CO alarm running.
 * Watch for condensation and mold on cold walls and windows. Wipe them dry and let a little air move.␀Fire-resistant materials␀fire resistant fireproof noncombustible materials brick stone clay cob sand metal cement board perlite vermiculite mineral wool fiberglass wool heat shield galvanized concrete spalling␀Knowing what burns, what melts and what holds up helps you build safe stoves, hearths, shields and fire pits, and dress safely near a fire.

== Non-combustible (won't burn)
Material | Good for | Watch out
Firebrick | Lining fireboxes, rocket stoves, ovens | Handles direct flame best
Common clay brick | Stove bases, heat walls, rocket stoves, thermal mass | Can crack in direct, intense flame over time
Stone | Fire rings, hearths, heat walls, thermal mass | Use only dry stone. Wet or river rock can burst in fire.
Clay, cob (clay + sand + straw), adobe | Ovens, stove surrounds, mass heaters, plaster over wood | Must dry fully before firing, or it cracks
Sand, gravel, earth | Hearth beds, insulating fill, putting out fires, bottom of barrel stoves | Keep sand dry for insulation
Cement board (Durock, HardieBacker) | Heat shields, hearth pads, wall protection | Use with a 1-inch air gap for shielding
Steel, cast iron | Stoves, pipe, pots, shields | Gets very hot and passes heat through. Thin steel can burn through.
Perlite, vermiculite | Lightweight insulation around rocket stove risers and ovens | Dusty, so wear a mask when pouring
Mineral wool (rock wool) | High-heat insulation near stoves and chimneys | Itchy, so wear gloves and a mask
Wood ash | Cheap insulating fill for rocket stoves and ovens | Make sure it's completely cold before using it
Ceramic tile | Hearth surface | Needs cement board behind it

== Resists fire but has limits
 * Concrete and cinder block: don't burn, but can crack, chip or pop violently (spall) when wet and heated hard. Fine as a base under a hearth, but don't build a fire directly on them.
 * Gypsum drywall: resists fire for a while (fire-rated "Type X" longer), but the paper face burns. Not a stove shield by itself.
 * Fiberglass insulation: the glass doesn't burn, but its paper or foil facing does, and it melts in high heat. Keep it away from stovepipe.
 * Aluminum: doesn't burn, but melts at around 1,200 degF, well within a wood fire's heat. Fine for foil reflectors, not fireboxes.
 * Glass: regular and tempered glass can crack or shatter from heat. Use only stove glass (ceramic) in stove doors.
 * Large, solid wood beams: char slowly on the outside, which protects the inside for a while. Small wood and boards burn fast.
 * Green (fresh) wood: hard to light because of its water, which is why living trees make poor firewood.
 * Clay slip: a coat of watery clay over straw or wood resists fire. Old-time builders used it on straw-and-clay walls.

== Never heat these
 * Galvanized steel (zinc-coated): toxic fumes when hot.
 * Painted, treated or chemical-soaked metal and wood: toxic smoke.
 * Plastic, foam board, vinyl: melts, drips burning, and gives off poisonous smoke.
 * Containers that held fuel or chemicals: can explode.

== Clothing near fire
 * Wool: naturally flame-resistant. It's hard to light, chars instead of melting, and tends to put itself out. The best fabric to wear around fire.
 * Leather: good for gloves and aprons when handling hot pots and wood.
 * Cotton and canvas: burn, but don't melt. Heavy canvas holds up to sparks better than thin cotton.
 * Fleece, nylon, polyester and other synthetics: melt and stick to skin when they catch, causing deep burns. Keep them away from the fire, or cover them with a wool or cotton outer layer.
 * Tie back long hair and loose sleeves when tending fires and stoves.␀Crossbow hunting␀crossbow bow archery hunting deer bolts broadhead scope shot placement tree stand michigan season quiet␀In Michigan, crossbows are legal for any licensed hunter in archery season (about Oct 1-Nov 14 and Dec 1-Jan 1 in 2026), and during regular firearm season. Rules change every year, so check the current Michigan DNR Hunting Digest. Hunter education is required for most new hunters.

== Why a crossbow
 * Easy to learn. It aims like a rifle with a stock and scope. Most people shoot good groups at 20-30 yards after a few sessions, much faster than learning a vertical bow.
 * Ready without moving. It stays cocked, so there's no drawing a bow while a deer watches. Less motion means fewer spooked animals.
 * Less strength needed. Rope cockers and crank cockers let teens, older hunters and people with shoulder problems use one.
 * Quiet. Much quieter than a gun. It doesn't spook every deer for a mile, and it doesn't announce to the neighborhood that you're hunting, which keeps a low profile in hard times.
 * Reusable ammo. Bolts can be found and shot again, and broadheads re-sharpened or blades replaced. No powder or primers to run out of.
 * Longer seasons. Michigan archery seasons run much longer than firearm season, giving more chances to fill the freezer.
 * Small game too: with field points or blunt "judo" tips for rabbits and squirrels.

== Limits
 * Range: most hunters should keep deer shots inside about 40 yards. Bolts drop and slow down past that.
 * Slow reload: you usually get one shot. Make it count.
 * Heavy and front-heavy to carry, and the string makes a "thwack" that can make deer jump.
 * The string, cables and rail need care and wear out.

== Safety
 * Fingers below the rail, always. The string can cut off fingers or thumbs above the rail. Use the foregrip.
 * Never dry-fire (shooting without a bolt). It can blow the crossbow apart. Use only the bolt weight and nock type the maker says.
 * Uncock it by shooting a field-point bolt into the ground or a target. Never climb into or out of a tree stand with it cocked.
 * Know what's beyond your target. Bolts fly far and pass through animals.
 * Keep limbs clear of trees and stand rails when shooting.
 * Tree stand falls are the top hunting injury. Wear a full-body safety harness, tied in from the moment you leave the ground. Raise and lower the crossbow, uncocked, with a haul rope.
 * Wear blaze orange when firearm seasons overlap.

== Gear and care
 * A scope sighted in at 20, 30 and 40 yards. Practice at known distances, then use a rangefinder or marked trees to judge distance in the field.
 * Broadheads for big game: practice with them, or with practice heads that fly the same, before the season. Keep them razor sharp.
 * Wax the string and rail lube as the manual says. Replace a fraying string or cables before they break.
 * Spare bolts, a cocking aid, a bolt puller, an Allen wrench set and string wax.

== Making a clean shot
 * Wait for a broadside or slightly quartering-away deer, standing still and unaware of you.
 * Aim at the heart-lung area: just behind the front leg, about a third of the way up the body. This kills quickly and humanely.
 * Skip head, neck, rear-end and running shots. They wound far more animals than they kill.
 * After the shot, mark where the deer stood and where you last saw it. Wait at least 30 minutes, and several hours for a shot that may have hit the gut. Then follow the blood trail slowly.
 * Bright, frothy blood means lungs, and the deer is usually close. Dark blood with food matter means gut, so back out and wait longer.

== Where and when
 * Deer move most at dawn and dusk, along edges between cover and food, and on trails between bedding and feeding areas.
 * Hunt with the wind blowing from the deer toward you. Deer smell far better than they see.
 * Sit still, scout ahead of time (tracks, rubs, scrapes, droppings), and stay on stand longer than feels comfortable.
 * Get permission for private land. Know the property lines.␀Trapping␀trap trapping snare foothold body grip conibear cage live trap raccoon muskrat beaver rabbit coyote fox fur license sets bait lure␀Traps work for you around the clock while you do other jobs. It's an old, efficient way to get meat and fur. Michigan requires a base license plus a fur harvester license, and sets seasons, trap sizes and how traps must be set and checked. Traps must carry your name. Read the current Michigan Furbearer Harvest Regulations, and take a trapper education course (the DNR and the Michigan Trappers & Predator Callers Association offer them).

== Main trap types
Type | Good for | Notes
Cage (live) trap | Raccoon, rabbit, squirrel, opossum, groundhog | Safest near homes. Lets you release pets and non-target animals unharmed.
Foothold trap | Coyote, fox, raccoon, mink, muskrat | Holds the animal by the foot. Takes skill to set and to choose the right size. Size limits apply.
Body-grip trap (Conibear type) | Muskrat, beaver, mink in water sets | Kills quickly. Dangerous to dogs and cats on land, so use mostly in water and away from pets. Land-set rules apply.
Snares and cable restraints | Regulated uses only | Michigan restricts where, when and for what species. Check the rules before using any.

== What's good to eat
 * Muskrat: clean, mild, dark meat. They eat plants.
 * Beaver: rich, dark, roast-like meat. The tail is fatty.
 * Raccoon: fatty, good slow-cooked. Remove the scent glands and cook it well.
 * Rabbit and squirrel: lean and mild, best stewed. Eat with fat.
 * Opossum and groundhog are edible too, cooked thoroughly.
 * Fox and coyote are usually trapped for fur and predator control, not meat.

== How to set traps well
 * Scout first. Look for tracks, trails, droppings, muddy slides into water, dens, and feeding signs. Set traps where animals already travel.
 * Hide your scent. New traps are oily. Boil them, dye and wax them (or let them weather), and handle them with clean gloves.
 * Common sets: a dirt-hole set (a small hole with bait, the trap bedded in front) for fox and coyote; a trail set where a path narrows; a water set at a muskrat run, beaver slide or den entrance; and cage traps baited along fence lines and building edges.
 * Bait and lure: sweet baits like marshmallows, apples and corn, or fish, for raccoons. Apple or carrot for muskrat and rabbit. Meat baits for predators. A good commercial lure helps.
 * Bed traps solidly so they don't wobble. Stake or anchor them firmly.
 * Mark every location on a map or with flagging, and count your traps in and out.

== Responsible trapping
 * Check traps often, at least daily, as the rules require. Morning is best.
 * Avoid places where dogs, cats and people walk, especially with body-grip traps on land.
 * Release non-target animals right away. Carry a catch pole or a release tool and gloves.
 * Dispatch quickly and humanely, by the methods taught in trapper education.
 * Wear gloves when handling animals. Raccoons, skunks, foxes and bats can carry rabies. Don't handle animals that act sick or strange; report them to the DNR.

== Fur
 * Prime fur comes in late fall and winter, which is when Michigan fur seasons are set.
 * Skin carefully, scrape off fat, and stretch the hide on a board or frame to dry. A dried pelt keeps for a long time.
 * Tanned hides make mittens, hats and blanket liners, and furs are good barter goods.

== Nuisance animals

Michigan lets landowners take certain nuisance animals (beavers, coyotes, muskrats, opossums, raccoons, skunks and weasels) that are damaging property, under specific conditions. Check the rules before acting.␀Field dressing & butchering game␀field dress gut skin butcher process deer venison rabbit squirrel raccoon age hang cuts quarter cwd gloves glands␀== Before you start
 * Bring a sharp knife, a spare, a small saw, gloves (long ones help), paper towels, rope, game bags and a sharpener.
 * Tag the animal and report it first as Michigan law requires.
 * Wear gloves. They protect against disease and keep the meat cleaner.
 * Chronic wasting disease (CWD) is in parts of Michigan. Get deer tested where it's offered, and don't eat a deer that tests positive or looked sick. Avoid cutting into the brain and spine, and don't eat the brain, spinal cord, eyes, spleen, tonsils or lymph nodes. Bone the meat out.

== Field dressing a deer
 * Roll it on its back, head slightly uphill.
 * Cut around the anus to free it, and tie it off with string so nothing spills.
 * Pinch up the belly skin and make a shallow cut. Slide two fingers in, blade between them facing up, and cut up to the breastbone without piercing the stomach or intestines.
 * Cut the diaphragm (the thin wall between belly and chest), reach up and cut the windpipe as far forward as you can.
 * Pull the organs out and down. Save the heart and liver if you want them; the liver should look smooth and evenly colored.
 * Drain the cavity, wipe it out, and prop it open to cool. Don't wash it with dirty water.

== Cool it fast
 * Heat spoils meat. Get it under 40 degF within a few hours.
 * Below 40 degF, hang the deer in shade, protected from animals, or quarter it and put it in game bags.
 * Above 40 degF, skin and quarter right away and put the meat on ice or in a cooler.
 * Aging: hanging at 34-40 degF for several days to a week makes meat more tender. Don't age in warm weather.

== Skinning and butchering
 * Skin while the animal is still warm if you can. The hide comes off much easier.
 * Main cuts: backstraps (loins along the spine, outside), tenderloins (inside, along the spine, take them early), hindquarters (roasts, steaks, jerky), and front shoulders, neck and trimmings (stew and ground meat).
 * Trim off all fat, silver skin, blood-shot meat and hair. Deer fat tastes waxy and goes rancid fast. Mix ground venison with pork or beef fat if you have it.
 * Don't cut through bone more than needed. Bone out the meat.
 * A deer gives very roughly 40-60 lb of boneless meat, depending on size.

== Small game
 * Rabbit: pinch and cut the skin at the middle of the back and pull both ways. Gut it. Check the liver: white or yellow spots can mean tularemia (rabbit fever). Discard the animal, and wear gloves.
 * Squirrel: cut under the tail, step on it, and pull the skin forward. Tough skin; it's a two-hand job. Gut it.
 * Raccoon: skin it and remove the small waxy, bean-sized scent glands under the front legs and inside the back legs. Trim the fat.
 * Muskrat and beaver: remove the musk glands near the tail carefully, without cutting into them.
 * Cool small game quickly in cold water or air.

== Clean up
 * Wash hands, knives and boards in hot soapy water, then sanitize with bleach water. On clean tools, a strong bleach soak (roughly 40% bleach, 5 minutes) is the advice for CWD.
 * Bury or dispose of remains as required, away from water, gardens and pets. Michigan has rules for deer carcass disposal.␀Cooking & storing wild game␀venison game meat store preserve freeze canning jerky smoke cure sausage pemmican tallow trichinella cook temperature␀== Cooking safely
 * Cook wild game to 165 degF, ground game to 160 degF or more, and game birds to 165 degF.
 * Bear, raccoon, wild pig and other meat-eaters can carry trichinella. Always cook them thoroughly. Freezing does not reliably kill the type found in wild game.
 * Venison is very lean. Cook steaks quickly and slow-cook tougher cuts (shoulder, shank) in liquid. Add fat when grinding.
 * Rabbit and squirrel are lean too. Stew them, and eat them alongside fats and carbohydrates. Lean meat alone leaves people weak.

== Freezing
 * Wrap tightly with no air: plastic wrap, then freezer paper, or a vacuum sealer. Air causes freezer burn.
 * Label with the cut, animal and date. It's best used within 9-12 months.
 * In a grid-down Michigan winter, a cooler kept outside in shade, safe from animals, works as a freezer. Watch for thaws.

== Canning (keeps for years, no power)
 * Pressure canner only. Meat is low-acid, so water-bath canning risks botulism.
 * Cut venison in 1-inch cubes or strips, trimmed of fat. Raw-pack into jars, add salt if you like, no liquid needed (it makes its own), leave 1 inch of space at the top.
 * The USDA process for cubed meat: pints 75 minutes, quarts 90 minutes, at 10 lb on a weighted-gauge canner or 11 lb on a dial gauge, adjusted for altitude. Follow the full USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning steps.
 * Canned venison is tender, ready to eat, and great for stews, tacos and gravy. Bones make broth to can too.

== Jerky (light, portable)
 * Lean meat sliced 1/4 inch thick against the grain, all fat trimmed. Marinate with salt, spices, vinegar or soy sauce.
 * Heat to 160 degF (in an oven or by steaming or boiling in marinade) before or after drying, then dry until it bends and cracks but doesn't snap.
 * It keeps 1-2 months sealed at room temperature, and longer frozen.

== Smoking and curing
 * Hot smoking (over about 200 degF) cooks and flavors meat. It still needs refrigeration or canning for long storage.
 * Cold smoking doesn't cook meat or make it safe by itself.
 * Curing sausage, ham and bacon needs curing salt (pink salt #1 or #2) in exact amounts. Too much is poisonous, too little risks botulism. Use a tested recipe only.
 * Salt-curing and drying (like old salt pork or dried fish) needs heavy salt and cold weather. Learn from someone experienced.

== Pemmican (long-lasting trail food)
 * Lean meat dried until it's brittle, then pounded or ground to a powder. Mix with roughly an equal weight of melted rendered fat, plus dried berries if you like.
 * Press it into bars or packs. It's very high in calories, and kept cool and sealed, it lasts many months.

== Rendering fat
 * Cut fat (beef, pork, bear, raccoon) into small pieces and melt slowly over low heat with a little water. Strain through cloth into jars.
 * Rendered fat (lard, tallow) keeps for months cool and sealed. Use it for cooking, pemmican, candles, soap and leather care.
 * Deer fat (tallow) isn't great for eating but makes candles and soap.

== Use the whole animal
 * Heart and liver: eat fresh (not from deer in CWD areas without testing). Bones: broth. Hide: leather or fur. Sinew: strong thread. Antlers: tools, handles, trade.
 * Scraps go to chickens (cooked) or the compost pit, buried deep so animals don't dig it up.␀Emergency childbirth␀childbirth birth baby delivery labor pregnant pregnancy cord placenta newborn bleeding midwife␀Most births go normally on their own. Your job is to keep mother and baby warm, clean and calm, and to let nature do the work. Find the most skilled help you can (midwife, nurse, doctor, an experienced mother) as early as possible. If you're expecting, ask your provider now what to do if you can't reach them.

== Get ready
 * A warm, clean, private space. Clean sheets or towels, plastic underneath, extra blankets.
 * Wash hands well and wear gloves if you have them.
 * Clean string or shoelaces boiled for 10 minutes, a new razor blade or boiled scissors, clean towels, a hat or cloth for the baby, pads, a bowl for the afterbirth.
 * Let the mother choose the position that feels best: kneeling, on hands and knees, side-lying or half sitting.

== The birth
 * Don't pull the baby. Support the head as it comes out, gently.
 * Once the head is out, feel for the cord around the neck. If it's there, gently slip it over the head.
 * The shoulders come next, usually with the next push. Support the baby as it slides out. Babies are slippery; hold firmly.
 * Note the time.

== The baby
 * Dry the baby right away with a warm towel, then lay it skin to skin on the mother's chest and cover both. Put a hat on the baby.
 * The baby should cry or breathe within a minute. If not, rub the back firmly and wipe the mouth and nose. A baby that still isn't breathing needs infant CPR, so learn it in a class now.
 * Help the baby start nursing. This helps the womb clamp down and slows the mother's bleeding.

== The cord and afterbirth
 * No hurry to cut the cord. Waiting a few minutes until it stops pulsing is good for the baby. If help is coming soon, it's fine to leave it attached.
 * To cut it: tie the cord tightly in two places a few inches apart, a hand's width or more from the baby, and cut between the ties with the clean blade.
 * The afterbirth (placenta) usually comes out within 30 minutes. Don't pull on the cord. Keep it in a bowl so a skilled person can check it's complete.
 * Gently but firmly rub the mother's lower belly, where you feel a firm ball (the womb), to help it tighten and stop the bleeding.

== Danger signs: get skilled help urgently
 * Heavy bleeding: soaking a pad in under an hour, or a steady flow.
 * The cord comes out before the baby. Have the mother get on her knees and chest, hips high, and get help.
 * The baby's bottom or feet come first, or the baby is stuck after the head.
 * Fever, foul-smelling discharge, severe headache, seizures or confusion in the days after.
 * A baby that is limp, blue, not feeding, or too cold.␀Chronic conditions without a pharmacy␀chronic diabetes insulin blood sugar low high blood pressure heart oxygen cpap dialysis asthma seizure thyroid medication stop suddenly␀People with long-term conditions are at highest risk when pharmacies and power are down. Plan now with their doctor: extra supply, written instructions, and what to do if medicine runs short.

== Never stop these suddenly without medical advice

Seizure medicines, some heart and blood pressure medicines (like beta blockers), steroids (like prednisone), some antidepressants and anxiety medicines, and opioid pain medicines. Stopping them abruptly can be dangerous. Ask the doctor now how to stretch or taper safely if needed.

== Diabetes
 * Keep insulin cool, never frozen (see Medicines). Have extra strips, lancets, pen needles and syringes, and a backup meter.
 * Low blood sugar (shaky, sweaty, confused, irritable): give about 15 grams of fast sugar (4 oz juice or regular soda, 4 glucose tablets, a tablespoon of sugar or honey). Recheck in 15 minutes and repeat if still low, then have a snack. If they can't swallow safely, don't put food in their mouth. Glucagon, if prescribed, is the treatment.
 * High blood sugar (very thirsty, peeing a lot, tired, fruity breath, vomiting) can become dangerous, especially in type 1. Drink water and follow the sick-day plan from the doctor.
 * Eat regular meals and keep sugar sources handy. Hard work burns sugar.
 * Check feet daily. Small wounds heal slowly and can turn serious.

== Blood pressure and heart
 * Keep taking medicine. A manual blood pressure cuff works without power.
 * Cut down on salt (hard when eating canned food; rinse canned vegetables and beans).
 * Know heart attack and stroke signs (see the emergency cards).

== Breathing
 * Asthma and COPD: extra inhalers, a spacer. Keep them out of smoke from stoves and fires.
 * Oxygen: concentrators need power. Keep backup oxygen cylinders and a battery or generator plan. Ask the supplier about outage plans. Keep oxygen away from all flames.
 * CPAP: many run on a 12-volt adapter or a battery pack. Turning off the heated humidifier makes the battery last much longer.

== Kidney dialysis
 * Ask your dialysis center now for its emergency plan and its emergency diet (it limits fluids, potassium and salt to stretch time between treatments).
 * Keep the center's backup locations and phone numbers on paper.

== Other
 * Thyroid, blood thinners, seizure medicines: extra supply, and know the signs of trouble (bleeding for blood thinners).
 * Allergies: two epinephrine pens per person who needs them, kept with the person.
 * Mental health: extra supply. Routine, sleep and support matter even more. Watch for relapse or crisis.
 * Mobility: spare wheelchair tires and tubes, a manual backup for powered chairs, a plan for stairs.␀Dental emergencies␀teeth tooth dental toothache abscess filling knocked out broken tooth gums dentist␀* Toothache: rinse with warm salt water (1 tsp salt in a cup of water), floss gently to remove stuck food, and take ibuprofen or acetaminophen as labeled. Clove oil on a cotton ball eases pain briefly. Don't put aspirin directly on the gum; it burns.
 * Lost filling or crown: a temporary filling kit (sold at drugstores) seals the hole. Keep one in the kit. A crown can be placed back with denture adhesive or temporary cement until a dentist can fix it.
 * Broken tooth: rinse, use a cold compress for swelling, and cover sharp edges with dental wax or sugarless gum.
 * Knocked-out adult tooth: pick it up by the top (not the root), rinse it briefly, and try to push it back into the socket and bite on gauze. If you can't, keep it in milk or in the person's cheek. The best chance is getting it back in within an hour. Don't replant baby teeth.
 * Abscess (painful swelling, pus, fever, bad taste): salt-water rinses, pain medicine, and skilled help as soon as possible. Swelling spreading into the face, eye, neck or under the tongue, or trouble swallowing or breathing, is an emergency.
 * Bleeding after a tooth comes out: bite firmly on gauze or a damp tea bag for 30 minutes.
 * Prevention is everything: brush twice a day, floss, cut back on sugar, and store extra toothbrushes, floss, toothpaste and a temporary filling kit.␀If you lose the house: emergency shelter␀shelter emergency tarp tent camping winter lean-to a-frame car sleeping homeless displaced evacuation snow quinzhee␀== First choices
 * Family, friends, neighbors, a church or a public shelter. Shared shelter is safer and warmer than going it alone.
 * A damaged house may still have one safe, dry room. Check that the structure is sound, with no gas smell or electrical danger, before going back in.
 * A garage, shed, barn or camper can be made livable with tarps, insulation and a safe heat source.

== Choosing a spot outdoors
 * High enough to stay out of water, but out of the wind (the lee side of a hill or trees).
 * No dead trees or big dead branches overhead ("widowmakers").
 * Close to water and firewood, away from roads, trails and flood paths.

== Tarp shelters
 * Lean-to: tie a ridge line between two trees, drape the tarp over it and stake one side to the ground, open side away from the wind. A fire in front reflects heat in.
 * A-frame: the tarp over a ridge line with both sides staked down. Sheds rain and snow well.
 * Pitch it tight so rain runs off, with the low end facing the wind.
 * Always put insulation under you: a foam pad, a thick layer of dry leaves, pine boughs, or cardboard. The ground pulls heat away faster than air.

== Tents and winter camping
 * A 4-season tent handles snow and wind. A 3-season tent with a tarp over it works in milder cold.
 * Sleeping system: two pads (foam plus inflatable) for winter, a bag rated well below the expected low, and a liner. Wear dry clothes and a hat to bed.
 * Crack a vent to cut condensation. Never bring a stove, grill or heater into a closed tent.
 * Pack snow around the base of the tent to block wind.
 * Snow shelter (quinzhee): pile and pack snow into a big mound, let it set for an hour or two, then hollow it out with walls about a foot thick. Poke a vent hole in the roof. It stays near freezing inside however cold it is outside. Never dig into snowbanks next to roads, where plows pass.

== A car as shelter
 * A car blocks wind and rain, but loses heat fast.
 * Run the engine for about 10 minutes an hour for heat, only with the tailpipe clear of snow, and crack a window on the side away from the wind. CO kills in cars.
 * Use blankets, sleeping bags and sunshades over the windows at night. Everyone together in the back.
 * Keep a winter kit in the car: blankets, water, food, a shovel, a flashlight, hand warmers, a candle in a can for light, and a bright cloth to tie on the antenna.

== Moving the family
 * Pack the go-bags, documents, meds, water and food. Leave a note saying where you went.
 * Travel in daylight, together, and on known routes. Stop before dark to set up camp.␀Knots, rope & cordage␀knots knot rope cord cordage bowline clove hitch square knot taut-line trucker's hitch sheet bend figure eight prusik timber hitch lashing paracord plant fiber␀Ten knots cover almost everything: tying down loads, hanging tarps and food, rescue lines and building. Practice them until you can tie them in the dark with cold hands.
Knot | Use it for | How it goes
Square knot | Tying two ends of the same rope, bandages, bundles | Right over left and under, then left over right and under. If it looks lopsided, it's a granny knot and will slip.
Bowline | A loop that won't slip or jam: rescue, tying to a post, hanging food | Make a small loop in the standing part. The end comes up through the loop ("rabbit out of the hole"), around the back of the standing part ("around the tree") and back down through the loop.
Clove hitch | Quick tie to a pole, starting lashings | Wrap around the pole, cross over your first wrap, wrap again and tuck the end under that second wrap.
Two half hitches | Tying a line to a ring, post or tree | Around the post, then two loops around the standing part in the same direction.
Taut-line hitch | Tent and tarp guy lines you can tighten | Around the stake, then two wraps around the standing line inside the loop, and one wrap outside. Slides to adjust and holds under load.
Trucker's hitch | Cinching loads and ridge lines very tight | Make a fixed loop partway up the line, run the end around the anchor and back through the loop, pull hard (it works like a pulley), then lock with two half hitches.
Sheet bend | Joining two ropes of different thickness | Bend the thicker rope into a U. Pass the thin end up through the U, around behind both legs, and tuck it under itself.
Figure-eight (and figure-eight loop) | A stopper at a rope end; a strong loop for climbing and rescue | Loop the end around the standing part and back through the first loop, making an 8.
Prusik | A grip knot that slides on a bigger rope, then holds. Adjustable tarp ties, climbing a rope. | Wrap a loop of thin cord around the main rope three times, passing through itself each time.
Timber hitch | Dragging logs, starting a diagonal lashing | Around the log, then twist the end around itself 3-4 times. Pulling tightens it.

== Lashings (for building)
 * Square lashing: joins two poles that cross at right angles, for frames, tables and racks. Start with a clove hitch, wrap 3-4 times around both poles, then "frap" (wrap between the poles) 2-3 times to tighten, and finish with a clove hitch.
 * Diagonal lashing: poles that cross at an angle and want to spring apart, like braces. Start with a timber hitch.
 * Shear lashing: two or three poles side by side, spread into an A-frame or tripod.

== Rope care
 * Keep rope dry and out of sun when stored. Coil it neatly.
 * Melt the ends of synthetic rope, or wrap (whip) the ends of natural rope with thin cord, so it doesn't unravel.
 * Don't step on rope or drag it through grit, which cuts fibers. Retire rope that's frayed, stiff or damaged for anything that holds a person.

== Making cordage
 * Paracord: the 7 inner strands pull out for sewing, fishing line, snare cord and small lashings.
 * Plastic bags and old clothes: cut into long strips and twist or braid them.
 * Plant fibers: dogbane, milkweed and nettle stalks (fall, when dry), and the inner bark of basswood, cedar and willow. Strip the fibers and soak bark until soft.
 * Reverse-wrap twist: fold a fiber bundle in half, twist the top strand away from you, then wrap it back over the other strand toward you, and repeat. The two strands lock into strong two-ply cord. Splice in new fibers as you go.
 * Braiding: three strands braided make flat, strong cord.␀Making & mending␀Soap making␀soap lye wood ash potash tallow lard make soap saponify caustic␀Soap keeps people healthy. It's worth storing plenty, and worth knowing how to make more. Lye is caustic: it burns skin and blinds eyes. Wear gloves and eye protection, keep kids and pets away, and keep vinegar and water nearby to rinse skin.

== Old-fashioned soap from wood ash
 * Make lye water: fill a wooden barrel or bucket with hardwood ash (white ash from clean, hot fires, no trash or treated wood). Put a layer of straw and gravel at the bottom over a small drain hole. Slowly pour rainwater through and catch the brown liquid that drips out. Run it through again to make it stronger.
 * Test the strength: it's traditionally ready when a fresh egg or small potato floats with a patch about the size of a quarter showing.
 * Cook it: simmer the lye water with rendered fat (lard or tallow) in an iron or stainless pot, never aluminum, stirring until it thickens like pudding ("trace"). This takes hours.
 * Ash lye makes a soft, jelly-like soap. Store it in crocks or jars and use it for laundry, dishes and washing.

== Modern bar soap
 * Uses store-bought lye (sodium hydroxide), fats and water in exact amounts. Use a tested recipe and a lye calculator. Too much lye burns skin.
 * Always add lye to water, never water to lye. It heats fast and can boil over. Do it outdoors or by an open window, since the fumes sting.
 * Mix with melted fats at the right temperature, stir to trace, pour into molds, and cure 4-6 weeks before using.

== Stretching soap
 * Grate bar soap and dissolve it in hot water for liquid soap. A little goes a long way for laundry. Save slivers in a mesh bag.␀Candles, oil lamps & light␀candles candle making tallow beeswax wick oil lamp olive oil lard lamp jar light␀* Dipped candles: melt tallow or beeswax in a tall can set in hot water. Dip a cotton wick, let it cool, and repeat 20-30 times until the candle is thick. Beeswax burns cleanest and longest; tallow smells a little.
 * Poured candles: melt wax and pour it into jars or cans around a wick held up by a pencil. Jar candles are much safer.
 * Wicks: braided 100% cotton string works. Soaking it in a strong salt or borax solution and drying it helps it burn evenly.
 * Oil lamp in a jar: a jar with an inch or two of olive or vegetable oil, and a cotton wick held up by a bent wire or a small piece of tin with a hole so the tip sits just above the oil. Burns for hours with little smoke, and the flame goes out if the jar tips into the oil.
 * Lard lamp: a cotton wick pushed into a can of solid shortening or lard burns for many hours.
 * Never use gasoline or other volatile fuels in homemade lamps.
 * Treat every flame as a fire risk: set it on a plate in a stable spot, away from curtains and kids, and never leave it burning unattended.
 * Mirrors or foil behind a candle or lamp double the light.␀Mending clothes & shoes␀sewing mend patch darn button stitch clothes shoes boots repair needle thread awl␀== Kit

Needles (several sizes), strong thread (heavy-duty, button and carpet thread, dental floss), safety pins, buttons, patches, scissors, a thimble, an awl, waxed thread, Shoe Goo or contact cement, and duct tape.

== Basic stitches
 * Running stitch: in and out in a straight line. Fast, for seams that don't take strain.
 * Backstitch: each stitch goes back into the end of the last one. Strong, like a machine seam. Use it for torn seams.
 * Whipstitch: loops over an edge. Good for patches and hems.
 * Knot the thread at the start and finish with several small stitches in one spot.

== Common repairs
 * Buttons: sew through 4-6 times, then wrap the thread around the stitches under the button to make a short "stem" and knot it.
 * Holes and tears: patch on the inside for small holes and the outside for big ones. Turn the patch edges under and whipstitch all around.
 * Socks: darn by weaving thread back and forth across the hole, then over and under those threads the other way. Wool socks last much longer with darning.
 * Knees and elbows: sew on patches before they wear through.
 * Zippers: rub stuck zippers with a pencil or wax. A loose slider can be pinched tighter with pliers.

== Shoes and boots
 * Glue loose soles with Shoe Goo or contact cement and clamp overnight.
 * Stitch torn leather with an awl and waxed thread.
 * Keep leather clean and oiled (rendered fat or wax) so it doesn't crack. Dry boots slowly, not next to the stove, which ruins leather.
 * Good boots and wool socks are among the most important things to keep in good repair.

== Make clothes last

Wear old clothes for dirty work. Wash less often and more gently, and air things out between wears. Save worn-out clothes for patches, rags, bandages and rope.␀Planning & practice␀Michigan month-by-month calendar␀calendar month season michigan year plan january february march april may june july august september october november december maple morels deer season planting␀Rough dates for southern Michigan. Check the DNR for exact hunting and fishing seasons each year.
Month | What to do
January | Stay warm. Check stores and rotate. Cut and split wood on frozen ground. Order seeds. Ice fishing. Repair tools. Late archery deer ends Jan 1.
February | Start onions and slow seeds indoors. Prune fruit trees. Tap maples late in the month when days thaw and nights freeze.
March | Maple syrup season. Start tomatoes, peppers and cabbage indoors. Clean the coop. Plan the garden. Rabbit and fur seasons wind down.
April | Plant peas, spinach, lettuce, radish, onions. Spring turkey season. Fix fences. Trout opener on the last Saturday. Chicks and new hens.
May | Plant potatoes, then beans, corn, squash and tomatoes after the last frost (mid-May). Hunt morels. Set up rain barrels.
June | Weed, mulch and water. Plant more beans and corn. Strawberries. Fishing. Cut next winter's wood so it dries all summer.
July | Harvest garlic. Can and freeze early crops. Berries. Plant fall carrots, beets and kale. Watch for heat and storms.
August | Big canning month: tomatoes, beans, corn, pickles. Save seeds. Dry herbs. Stack wood under cover.
September | Harvest winter squash, potatoes, apples. Fill the root cellar. Small game seasons open mid-month. Check chimneys and stoves.
October | Plant garlic. Archery deer opens Oct 1. Gather nuts. Bank the foundation. Insulate windows. Fur seasons start.
November | Firewood in. Firearm deer season Nov 15-30. Butcher, can and smoke meat. Winterize pipes, cars and the coop.
December | Late archery deer (Dec 1-Jan 1). Trapping. Teach and mend indoors. Plan next year's garden and supplies. Celebrate together.

Every season: rotate food and water, test smoke and CO alarms, run the generator, check medicines and go-bags.␀Practice drills␀drill practice test lights out weekend exercise checklist rehearse evacuation fire drill␀Plans that haven't been tested usually fail in some small way. Practice now, while mistakes are cheap.

== Lights-out weekend
 * Pick a weekend. Shut off the main breaker (or just stop using power, if the freezer is full) and the water at the main valve for a day.
 * Live on your supplies: cook without power, use your water storage and the bucket toilet, light with lanterns, heat with your backup plan.
 * Keep a notebook. Write down everything that was missing, broken, hard or confusing.
 * Afterward, fix the list: buy what was missing, repair what broke, and update this app.

== Quick drills (every few months)
 * Fire: everyone out of bed to the meeting spot. Time it.
 * Grab and go: can everyone be in the car with go-bags, meds and pets in 10 minutes?
 * Shelter in place: everyone to the safe room. Is the water, light and radio there?
 * Radio check-in: each family member on their radio from their spot in the house and yard, then the neighbors.
 * First aid scenario: act out a bad cut or a fall. Who grabs the kit, who stops bleeding, who calls for help?
 * Gas and water shutoff: each adult finds the valves and shows how they work (don't actually shut off the gas).
 * Meeting spot: walk or bike to the backup meeting spot.

== Make it fun for kids

Call it camping at home. Give them jobs: lantern keeper, water counter, radio operator, cook's helper. Let them help write the "what we learned" list.␀When someone dies␀death dead body died burial grief remains funeral record␀In a long emergency, a family may have to care for someone who has died before help can come. It's hard, and it's an act of respect. Follow official instructions whenever authorities can be reached.

== Right away
 * Make sure the person has died: no breathing, no pulse, no response.
 * Write down their name, date and time, place, what happened, and who was there. Keep any ID with them.
 * Report the death to authorities (police, the medical examiner, a hospital or the emergency management office) as soon as you can reach them.

== Caring for the body
 * Wear gloves. Wash your hands afterward.
 * Bodies of people who died from injuries or accidents pose little disease risk to others. Those who died of an infectious disease (like cholera or a pandemic illness) need more care: gloves, masks, minimal handling, and washing everything after.
 * Lay the person flat, close the eyes and mouth, wrap them in a clean sheet or body bag, and label it with their name and date.
 * Keep the body in the coolest place available, away from the living space, from animals and from water sources.

== If burial can't wait
 * Only when officials can't be reached for a long time. Laws normally require reporting, permits and a death certificate.
 * Bury well away from water: guidance is at least about 650 feet (200 m) from streams and lakes and 800 feet (250 m) from drinking wells, on higher ground.
 * Dig deep enough that animals can't reach the body.
 * Mark the grave and record its exact location on a map, with the person's name, so the family and authorities can find it later.

== Grief
 * Hold a simple service with prayers, words, songs or a candle, even if it's small. Rituals help people carry on.
 * Give people time and space to grieve, and lighten their work for a few days.
 * Watch over children and anyone hit hardest. Talk about the person and keep their memory.␀Running Rugged on a Raspberry Pi␀raspberry pi 400 computer offline battery power bank low power screen monitor sd card backup app␀Rugged works with no internet once it's on a device. A Raspberry Pi 400 uses very little power, so it can keep this guide, your household lists and your plans running through an outage on a small battery.

== Power
 * The Pi 400 itself draws very roughly 3-6 watts. The screen usually uses more than the Pi.
 * Screen: a small USB-C portable monitor uses far less than a TV. Turn brightness down and use Night mode.
 * Power bank: it must supply a steady 5 volts at 3 amps over USB-C. Weak banks cause a lightning-bolt "low voltage" warning and crashes. A 20,000 mAh bank can run the Pi for many hours.
 * 12-volt battery (from solar or the Pedal Power Station): use a good-quality 12 V to 5 V, 3 A USB-C converter, fused at the battery.
 * Run it only when needed. Look things up, then shut down.

== Keep it healthy
 * Always shut down from the menu (Raspberry menu, then Shutdown), and wait for the light to stop before pulling power. Pulling power while running can corrupt the memory card.
 * Keep a backup: copy the My Apps folder to a USB stick, and back up your suite work with Snug.
 * Your saved work lives in the Pi's web browser. Don't clear browser data.
 * Keep it dry, dust-free and away from the stove.
 * Print the guide too (Print button on this tab). Paper never runs out of battery.␀Power budget: how much gear draws␀power budget draw watts watt-hours amps radio comms communication starlink router gmrs ham hf cb handheld transmit receive inverter battery calculator usage␀Planning power is simple arithmetic: watts x hours = watt-hours (Wh) per day. Add up your gear, compare it to your battery and solar, and you'll know how long you can last. The calculator on the Supplies tab does this for you.

== Quick conversions
 * Watts = volts x amps. A 12 V device drawing 2 A uses 24 W.
 * Battery Wh = amp-hours x volts. A 100 Ah 12 V battery holds about 1,200 Wh (about 1,280 Wh for a 12.8 V lithium).
 * Labels on chargers and devices list volts and amps, often as "input" and "output." The output is closer to what the device actually uses.
 * A plug-in power meter (Kill A Watt type) or a USB power meter shows the real draw. Worth owning.

== Everyday gear (typical draw while on)
Device | Watts | Notes
LED bulb | 5-10 | A 60-watt-equivalent LED is about 8-9 W
LED lantern / headlamp | 1-5 | Runs days on AAs
Phone charge | 10-20 Wh per full charge | Charge from 12 V or USB directly
Tablet / e-reader | 5-10 / under 1 | E-readers last weeks
Laptop | 30-60 | Lower brightness and close extra apps
Raspberry Pi + 7" screen | 5-6 | See Running Rugged on a Raspberry Pi
TV (32") | 30-60 | Big TVs use far more
Box fan | 50-100 | 12 V DC fans use 5-20 W
CPAP | 30-60 | 60-100+ with heated humidifier on
Electric blanket / heating pad | 50-100 | Heats a person far cheaper than heating a room
Refrigerator | About 1-2 kWh a day | Starting surge 3-5x running watts
Chest freezer | About 0.5-1.5 kWh a day | Keep it full and closed
Gas furnace blower | 300-800 | Needs a pure sine inverter or generator
Well pump | 700-2,000+ | Big starting surge; fill jugs in one session
Sump pump | 500-1,000 | Only while running
Microwave, coffee maker, hair dryer, space heater | 900-1,500 | Too much for most battery setups

== Communications gear

Radios draw very little while listening and a lot while talking. Most of the day is listening, so the listening number matters most. Talk time is usually a small fraction.
Gear | Listening | Talking | Notes
AM/FM/NOAA weather radio | 1-3 W | n/a | Hand-crank and solar models need no batteries
Shortwave receiver | 1-3 W | n/a | Listen to news from far away
Handheld FRS / GMRS / ham walkie-talkie | Under 0.5 W | About 5-10 W | Days per charge or set of AAs with short talks
CB radio (mobile) | About 5-7 W | About 20-30 W | Runs straight from 12 V
Mobile GMRS or 2 m ham (up to 50 W out) | About 7-15 W (0.5-1 A at 13.8 V) | About 120-150 W (about 10 A) | Turn transmit power down to save battery
HF ham radio (100 W out) | About 20-30 W (1.5-2 A) | About 250-300 W (about 20 A) | Long distance. Needs a stout 12 V battery and wire
Wi-Fi router / mesh unit | 6-15 W |  | Most run on 12 V with the right cable
Starlink Mini | About 25-40 W |  | Runs on 12 V with an adapter
Starlink Standard | About 50-75 W |  | More in snow-melt mode. A big daily load
Cell signal booster | 5-10 W |  | Only helps if towers still have power
Laptop for radio messages | 30-60 W |  | A Pi uses a tenth of that

== Example: a small comms station for one day
Item | Math | Wh/day
Mobile GMRS, listening 12 hours | 10 W x 12 h | 120
Mobile GMRS, talking 20 minutes | 140 W x 0.33 h | 47
NOAA radio 4 hours | 2 W x 4 h | 8
Two handhelds recharged | 2 x 6 Wh | 12
Raspberry Pi + screen 3 hours | 6 W x 3 h | 18
Total | About 205

A 1,000 Wh lithium power station runs that for about 4 days with no sun. A single 100 W solar panel on a good Michigan summer day roughly keeps up.

== Stretching the power
 * Use 12 V directly when you can. Inverters waste 10-15% converting to AC, and many use 10-30 W just sitting on. Turn the inverter off when not in use.
 * Radios: keep transmissions short, use low power for nearby contacts, and turn the radio off between scheduled check-ins.
 * Schedule the big loads: run the fridge, well pump or freezer in blocks, on generator time or at midday when solar is strongest.
 * Surge: motors (fridges, pumps, furnace blowers) need 3-5 times their running watts for a second when starting. Size the inverter or generator for that.
 * Battery reality: use about 90% of a lithium (LiFePO4) battery, but only about 50% of a lead-acid one if you want it to last.
 * Cold: batteries give less in the cold, and lithium must not be charged below freezing. Keep the battery bank in the warm room.
 * Write your real numbers into the calculator after a lights-out weekend.␀Antennas you can build␀antenna improvised dipole ground plane j-pole cb 11 meter 10 meter 27 mhz 2 meter gmrs coax swr long wire shortwave yagi tape measure height␀The antenna matters more than the radio. A simple homemade antenna up high beats an expensive radio with a stubby antenna down low. These designs are classic and well-tested.

== The two formulas
 * Half-wave dipole, total length in feet = 468 / frequency in MHz. Each side is half of that.
 * Quarter-wave vertical in feet = 234 / frequency in MHz. Multiply by 12 for inches.
 * Cut it a little long, then trim a bit at a time while checking with an SWR meter. Aim for an SWR under 2:1, ideally near 1.5:1 or lower. High SWR can damage a transmitter.
Band | Frequency | Dipole total | Quarter-wave vertical
CB (11 meters), ch. 19 | 27.185 MHz | About 17 ft 3 in (8 ft 7 in per side) | About 102 in (the classic CB whip)
10 meters (ham) | 28.4 MHz | About 16 ft 6 in | About 99 in
2 meters (ham) | 146 MHz | About 38 in | About 191/4 in
GMRS | 462.6 MHz | About 12 in | About 6 in
Shortwave listening | Many | A long wire, 20-100 ft, clipped to the radio's antenna

== Dipole (CB, 10 m and HF bands)
 * Two equal wires in a straight line with a small gap and an insulator in the middle. The coax center conductor connects to one wire and the shield to the other.
 * Insulators at both ends tie off to trees or poles with rope. Hang it as high as you can, at least a quarter-wavelength up if possible.
 * An inverted V (center high on one pole, ends sloping down) works nearly as well and needs only one support.
 * Wire: any copper, such as speaker wire pulled apart, house wire, or electric fence wire in a pinch.

== Ground plane (vertical, for CB, 2 m, GMRS base)
 * A quarter-wave vertical wire or rod connected to the coax center, plus 3-4 "radials" (each about 5% longer than the vertical) connected to the coax shield, sloping down at about 45 deg.
 * A 2 m or GMRS ground plane can be built on an SO-239 panel connector with stiff copper wire in an hour. Mount it high on a pole.

== Other proven designs
 * J-pole: a vertical antenna for 2 m made from copper pipe or 300-ohm twin-lead "ladder line." Easy, sturdy and very popular. Use published dimensions.
 * Tape-measure Yagi: a directional 2 m antenna made from steel tape measure pieces on a PVC frame. Points at a distant repeater or station.
 * Long wire for shortwave receiving: as long and high as you can, insulated at the far end. Greatly improves listening at no cost.

== Coax and connections
 * Use 50-ohm coax. RG-8X or LMR-400 have much less loss than thin RG-58, which matters at GMRS and 2 m. Keep runs short.
 * TV coax (75-ohm RG-6) works in a pinch, especially for receiving and HF, with a small mismatch.
 * Seal outdoor connectors with tape and coax sealant. Water in coax ruins it.

== Safety and rules
 * Stay far from power lines. If an antenna or rope could fall onto or touch a line, choose another spot. People die this way every year.
 * Disconnect antennas from radios during thunderstorms and ground the coax outside.
 * FRS radios must use their built-in antenna. GMRS radios that allow it can use external antennas.
 * CB: legal power is 4 W AM or 12 W SSB, no amplifiers, and there are height limits for CB antennas on structures.
 * 10 meters is next door to CB, and CB antennas often work there with a little trimming. Ham bands need a license. A Technician license includes some 10-meter privileges.␀Morse code & improvised signaling␀morse code cw sos table letters numbers signal flashlight mirror whistle tapping practice oscillator buzzer key transmitter␀Morse code gets through when voice won't: weak signals, noise, a flashlight across a valley, or tapping on a pipe. It's slow to learn well, but SOS and a few letters take an hour.

== Timing
 * A dot is 1 unit, a dash is 3 units.
 * Gap inside a letter: 1 unit. Between letters: 3 units. Between words: 7 units.
 * Learn by sound ("dit" and "dah"), not by counting dots on paper. "A" sounds like di-dah.
Letter | Code
A | --
B | ----
C | ----
D | ---
E | -
F | ----
G | ---
H | ----
I | --
J | ----
K | ---
L | ----
M | --
Letter | Code
N | --
O | ---
P | ----
Q | ----
R | ---
S | ---
T | -
U | ---
V | ----
W | ---
X | ----
Y | ----
Z | ----
Number | Code
1 | -----
2 | -----
3 | -----
4 | -----
5 | -----
6 | -----
7 | -----
8 | -----
9 | -----
0 | -----

== Signals worth knowing
 * SOS = --------- sent as one unbroken signal. Distress.
 * Three of anything (flashes, whistle blasts, gunshots where legal, fires) also means distress.
 * K = "over, go ahead." AR (-----) = end of message. SK (------) = signing off. BT (-----) = break, new thought.
 * R = "received." ? = please repeat.

== Sending without a radio
 * Flashlight or lantern: short flash = dot, long = dash. Covering and uncovering the light with a hand or board works too. At night, a flashlight can be seen for miles.
 * Signal mirror: in sunlight, flashes can be seen many miles away. Aim by holding two fingers in a V toward the target and keeping the bright spot on them.
 * Whistle or horn: short and long blasts. A whistle carries much farther than a voice and saves energy.
 * Tapping: on pipes, walls or rails. Trapped people should tap in threes. Searchers listen for it.
 * Ground-to-air: big letters on the ground, at least 10 ft tall, in contrasting colors. V = need help, X = need medical help,  = going this way.

== Sending with a radio
 * Any licensed voice radio can send Morse: key the talk button on and off in the dot-dash pattern, or whistle or hum the tones into the mic while holding the button.
 * HF ham radios have a real CW mode. A Morse key plugs into the back. CW gets through on very little power when voice can't.
 * Practice set (no transmitting, totally legal): a 9-volt battery, a small buzzer, and a homemade key (a clothespin or two strips of metal that touch when pressed), wired in a loop. Great for kids and for learning by sound.
 * Don't build homemade transmitters like old "spark-gap" sets. They splatter interference across many frequencies, can disrupt emergency radio, and are illegal. Licensed hams can build legal low-power transmitter kits.
 * Rules: transmitting on ham bands needs a license. In a true emergency, with life in immediate danger and no other way to call for help, any means of radio communication is allowed.␀Salvage & re-engineering␀salvage scavenge repurpose reuse parts junk re-engineer battery alternator motor generator ups inverter solar lights laptop charger old phone gps car radio treadmill␀A lot of useful gear is hiding in things that already exist. Look at broken or unused items for what they contain: batteries, motors, wire, magnets, lights, fans and chargers.

== What to look for
Find this | Get this | Use it for
Car or truck | 12 V battery, lights, wiring, fuses, fans, radio | Battery bank, lighting, a 12 V radio receiver for news, phone charging with a car charger
Computer UPS (battery backup box) | Sealed 12 V batteries, a small inverter | Runs a router, lights or a laptop. Recharge from a car or solar.
Solar garden lights | Tiny panel, AA rechargeable, LED | Night lights; charge AA batteries
Old phones and tablets | Screen, flashlight, camera, GPS | GPS works without cell service if maps are downloaded first. Store e-books, PDFs and this guide offline.
Laptop chargers, wall chargers | Steady DC power supplies | Powering 12-19 V gear from a generator or inverter
Treadmill, power tools | DC motor | A DC motor spun by a bike or wind makes electricity (with a diode and charge controller)
Car alternator | Generator | Bike, wind or water power projects. Needs high speed and a field connection.
Bicycle | Dynamo hub, chain, gears | Pedal generators, pumps, grain mills
Speakers, hard drives | Strong magnets, speaker wire | Antenna wire, pickup tools, compass needles
Old TVs, cable lines | Coax cable | Antenna feed lines (works in a pinch)
Washing machines, dryers | Motors, belts, pulleys, drums | Grain mill or pump drives; a drum makes a fire pit or stove liner (not galvanized)
Refrigerator | Insulated box | Root cellar, cold box, haybox cooker
Fans, computer fans | 12 V fans | Ventilation, stove blowers, food dryers
Christmas lights, LED strips | Low-power lights | 12 V or USB lighting

== How to re-engineer something
 * Read the label. Volts, amps, watts, AC or DC. That tells you what it needs and what it can give.
 * Match the power. 12 V gear on a 12 V battery. 5 V USB gear on a USB car charger. AC gear needs an inverter.
 * Fuse it. A fuse near the battery on every new circuit prevents fires.
 * Test small first, with a multimeter if you have one. Watch for heat, smell or smoke.
 * Write down what you did and label the wires.

== Dangerous to take apart: leave these alone
 * Microwave ovens, old tube TVs, camera flashes: capacitors inside hold a deadly charge even when unplugged.
 * Swollen or damaged lithium batteries: fire and explosion risk. Don't puncture, crush or heat them.
 * Refrigerant lines (fridges, AC units): gas under pressure.
 * Old insulation, tiles and pipe wrap that may contain asbestos.
 * Anything connected to house power. Turn off the breaker and test before touching.

== Ask before you take

Salvage from your own things, or with the owner's permission. A community salvage pile, where people bring broken items to share parts, is a great block project.␀Digital radio modes (PSK31 and more)␀digital modes psk31 ft8 js8call winlink rtty sstv sound card decode computer ham frequencies waterfall␀Digital modes send text over radio as audio tones that a computer decodes. They get through on very little power and in noise where voice fails. Anyone may listen and decode. Transmitting needs the right ham license.

== How to listen with Rugged
 * Tune a shortwave or ham receiver to a digital frequency (below) in USB mode.
 * Put the radio's speaker near the computer or Pi microphone, or run a cable from the radio's headphone jack to the computer's mic or line input. A cable is far better.
 * On the Comms tab, open the Digital mode monitor and press Listen. PSK31 signals look like thin, twin-line tracks on the waterfall.
 * Click the middle of a track. Rugged snaps to it and decodes the text. Keep AFC on so it follows small drift.
 * Turn the radio's volume so the waterfall background is dark blue, not bright. Too loud distorts.

== Common modes
Mode | What it is | Decode in Rugged?
PSK31 | Keyboard chat at typing speed. Narrow, efficient, very popular. | Yes
FT8 / FT4 | Short automated contact exchanges. Works in deep noise. Not for chatting. | No (use WSJT-X)
JS8Call | Free-text messaging built on FT8. Store-and-forward. Popular for emergency nets. | No (use JS8Call)
RTTY | Old-time radio teletype. Two-tone sound. | No (use fldigi)
Winlink | Email over radio, connects to the internet through gateway stations. Used by emergency groups. | No (Winlink Express)
SSTV | Pictures sent by sound. | No (use QSSTV or MMSSTV)

== Where to listen (USB, kHz)
Band | PSK31 | FT8
80 m | 3,580 | 3,573
40 m | 7,070 (also around 7,035-7,040) | 7,074
30 m | 10,142 | 10,136
20 m | 14,070 | 14,074
17 m | 18,100 | 18,100
15 m | 21,070 | 21,074
10 m | 28,120 | 28,074

20 m is busiest in daytime, 40 m and 80 m at night. fldigi (free) decodes almost every mode and runs on a Raspberry Pi.␀Nuclear attack & fallout␀nuclear bomb blast flash fallout radiation shelter emp potassium iodide decontaminate 7-10 rule protection␀A nuclear attack is survivable for most people outside the immediate blast area. The big danger afterward is fallout: radioactive dust that falls from the sky within minutes to hours. Sheltering correctly saves the most lives.

== If you see a blinding flash
 * Drop to the ground, face down, and cover your head and any bare skin. Don't look at the flash.
 * If there's a wall, ditch or car nearby, get behind it. Stay down until the blast wave and the wind pass, which can take up to a minute or more far from the blast.
 * Then get inside the nearest sturdy building fast, before fallout arrives (it can begin within about 10-15 minutes near the blast).

== Get inside, stay inside, stay tuned
 * Best shelter: a basement, or the middle floors and center of a large brick or concrete building. Put as much mass (walls, earth, floors) between you and the outside as you can.
 * Worst: cars, mobile homes and wood-frame rooms with lots of windows. Leave them for something better if it's only a few minutes away.
 * Close windows and doors and turn off fans, furnace and AC that pull in outside air.
 * Stay sheltered at least 24 hours unless officials tell you otherwise. Listen on a battery or hand-crank radio.
 * The 7-10 rule: fallout radiation drops about 90% after 7 hours and about 99% after 2 days. The first hours are the most dangerous, so don't leave early to find family. Agree now to shelter where you are.
 * Bring pets inside.

== Cleaning off fallout (decontamination)
 * If you were outside, take off your outer layer of clothing right away, before entering the living space. This can remove most of the contamination. Put it in a sealed plastic bag, away from people.
 * Shower with soap and shampoo. No conditioner, which binds radioactive material to hair. Don't scrub hard enough to break the skin.
 * No shower? Wipe exposed skin, hair and face with a damp cloth or wipes. Gently blow your nose and wipe your eyelids and ears.
 * Wipe off pets with a damp cloth, and wear gloves.

== Food, water and medicine
 * Food in sealed cans, jars and packages, and in the fridge or freezer, is safe. Wipe the outside of containers before opening.
 * Bottled water and water already in your pipes and water heater is safe.
 * Potassium iodide (KI) protects only the thyroid, and only from one type of radiation. Take it only when public health officials say to, at the dose they give. It isn't for everyone, and it's no substitute for shelter.

== Electromagnetic pulse (EMP)
 * A high-altitude blast can damage electronics over a wide area. Unplug sensitive electronics if there's warning.
 * Spare radios, a small solar charger, a phone and a flashlight wrapped in cloth and stored inside a closed metal can (a galvanized trash can or ammo can, with the items not touching the metal) are better protected.
 * Older vehicles and simple hand tools, crank radios and paper maps keep working.

== Prepare now
 * Know your best shelter spot at home, work and school.
 * Keep 2 weeks of water and food, a battery or crank radio, a first-aid kit and medicines where you'd shelter.
 * Know your local siren signals and emergency radio station.␀Chemical attack or chemical release␀chemical attack release spill gas nerve agent chlorine smell shelter in place upwind decontaminate eyes skin mask train derailment␀Chemical emergencies come from accidents (train derailments, plant leaks, tanker spills) far more often than attacks. The response is the same.

== Warning signs
 * A strange smell, a visible cloud, mist or oily droplets.
 * Several people suddenly coughing, choking, with watery eyes, twitching, trouble breathing, or collapsing.
 * Many dead or sick birds, fish, insects or small animals in one area.

== If you're outside
 * Move away from the source, upwind and uphill. Go crosswise to the wind rather than straight away from it if the cloud is moving toward you.
 * Cover your nose and mouth with cloth. It helps only a little, so keep moving.
 * Get inside the nearest building if you can't get clear.

== Shelter in place
 * Close and lock windows and doors. Turn off fans, furnace and AC, and close fireplace dampers.
 * Go to an inside room with the fewest windows, upstairs if possible. Many chemicals are heavier than air and settle low.
 * Seal gaps around doors, windows and vents with plastic sheeting and duct tape, or wet towels.
 * Bring water, a radio, a phone and the first-aid kit. Listen for the all-clear, then open windows and air the house out.

== If you're exposed
 * Get to fresh air first.
 * Remove clothing quickly. Cut off shirts rather than pulling them over your head. Bag them.
 * Wash skin and hair with lots of soap and water. Don't use bleach on skin.
 * Flush eyes with clean water for 10-15 minutes. Remove contact lenses.
 * Don't eat, drink or touch your face until you've washed.
 * Get medical help urgently for trouble breathing, tiny pupils, drooling, heavy sweating, muscle twitching, confusion or seizures. These need trained medical treatment.

== Masks

N95 and dust masks don't protect against chemical gases. Only properly fitted respirators with the right cartridges do, and they need training and fit-testing. Distance and shelter are your real protection.␀Drone threats␀drone drones uav attack surveillance cover concealment shelter thermal night buzzing downed drone report␀Drones are now common in conflicts, for both watching and attacking. Protection comes down to not being seen, getting under solid cover, and staying away from likely targets.

== Avoid being a target
 * Stay away from military sites, power plants, substations, fuel depots, bridges, rail yards, checkpoints and convoys.
 * Avoid crowds and gatherings in the open, and avoid parking groups of vehicles together in the open.
 * Travel in short moves between cover rather than across open ground. Fog, rain and low cloud limit drones.
 * Keep outdoor lights off at night and windows covered. Many drones carry thermal cameras that see warm bodies, engines and stoves in the dark, so solid roofs, walls and trees overhead help more than darkness.
 * Cover vehicles and keep them under carports, trees or in garages.

== If you hear or see a drone
 * A buzzing or whining like a lawn mower or swarm of insects overhead is a warning sign.
 * Get under solid overhead cover: inside a building, under a concrete overhang, into a basement, or at least under dense trees.
 * If caught in the open with no cover, lie flat in a ditch or low spot, face down, and stay still. Movement draws attention.
 * Inside, move away from windows and outside walls to an inner room or basement. Flying glass causes many injuries. Window safety film or boards over windows help. Tape on glass does little.
 * Follow official air-raid or drone alerts and stay sheltered until the all-clear.

== After a strike, or a downed drone
 * Never touch a downed drone or unexploded object. Many carry explosives or are rigged to explode. Keep everyone at least a few hundred feet away and report it to authorities.
 * Expect a second strike at the same spot. Don't crowd the scene.
 * Treat injuries: stop bleeding with pressure, packing or a tourniquet (see the emergency cards), and cover wounds.
 * Check for fire, gas leaks and structural damage before going back into a building.

== Good to know
 * Radio jammers are illegal for civilians in the U.S. They also block emergency radio and GPS for everyone nearby.
 * Keep a basement or inner room ready as your shelter, stocked like any storm shelter.␀Someone is bleeding badly␀Press hard on the wound with cloth and don't let up. Don't lift to peek. Pack deep wounds tightly with gauze or clean cloth, then press. For an arm or leg bleeding that won't stop, put a tourniquet 2-3 inches above the wound (not on a joint) and tighten until the bleeding stops. Write the time on it. Keep the person warm and lying down.␀Not breathing, no pulse (CPR)␀Shout for help. Push hard and fast in the center of the chest: 2 inches deep, 100-120 a minute (the beat of "Stayin' Alive"). If trained, give 2 breaths after every 30 pushes. Otherwise, push nonstop. Swap pushers every 2 minutes. Keep going until they breathe or you can't go on.␀Choking␀If they can cough or talk, let them cough. If they can't, stand behind them, put a fist just above the belly button, and give hard inward-and-up thrusts until it comes out. For a baby: 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades with the baby face-down on your forearm, then 5 two-finger chest pushes. Repeat.␀Heart attack signs␀Chest pressure or pain, pain in the arm, jaw or back, sweating, shortness of breath, nausea. Women often feel it as tiredness, nausea or back pain. Sit them down and keep them calm. If they're not allergic and have no bleeding problems, have them chew one regular 325 mg aspirin. Be ready for CPR.␀Stroke signs (FAST)␀Face drooping, Arm weak, Speech slurred: Time matters. Note when it started. Lay them on their side if they're vomiting or drowsy. Give nothing by mouth. Get the most skilled help you can find right away.␀Seizure␀Move hard things away and cushion the head. Don't hold them down and put nothing in their mouth. Time it. Afterward, roll them onto their side and let them rest. Over 5 minutes, or one seizure after another, is an emergency.␀Severe allergic reaction␀Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, trouble breathing, hives all over. Use their epinephrine pen in the outer thigh right away, through clothing is fine. A second dose after 5-15 minutes if not better. An antihistamine helps itching but doesn't stop a severe reaction.␀Broken bone␀Don't straighten it unless the hand or foot below is turning blue and cold. Splint it as it lies with boards, sticks or rolled magazines, padding it and tying above and below the break. Check fingers or toes for warmth and color. A bone through the skin: cover it with a clean wet dressing.␀Burn␀Cool with cool (not ice) running or poured water for 20 minutes. Take off rings and watches. Cover loosely with clean plastic wrap or cloth. No butter, oils or ice. Burns bigger than the person's palm, or on the face, hands or groin, are serious. Give fluids.␀Hypothermia␀Signs: shivering, fumbling, mumbling, stumbling, then confusion. Get them out of wind and wet. Remove wet clothes and wrap them in dry blankets with a hat. Warm the chest, neck, armpits and groin first with warm bottles or a body. Give warm sweet drinks only if fully awake. Handle severe cases very gently.␀Frostbite␀Skin is white, gray or waxy and numb. Don't rub it. Rewarm only if it won't freeze again, in warm (not hot) water about 100-104 degF for 20-30 minutes. It will hurt. Pad between fingers and toes and don't pop blisters.␀Heat stroke␀Hot skin, confusion, fainting, maybe no sweat. Cool them fast: shade, strip, soak with water and fan, ice or cold packs at the neck, armpits and groin. Sips of water only if alert. This can kill within hours.␀CO alarm, headache or dizziness␀Everyone out to fresh air now. Leave the doors open. Shut off the generator, grill or stove. Sleepy, confused or vomiting people need help first. Several people with headaches at once means CO until proven otherwise.␀Smell gas␀Don't flip switches, light anything or start a car. Get everyone out. Shut the gas off at the meter (quarter turn, so the valve runs crosswise to the pipe) only if it's safe. Once off, only the gas company should turn it back on.␀Fire in the house␀Get everyone out and stay out. Crawl low under smoke. Feel doors with the back of your hand before opening. Only fight a fire smaller than a wastebasket, with a clear way out behind you. Smother a grease fire with a lid and never use water on it.␀Power line down␀Stay at least 35 feet away, about two car lengths, and treat it as live. If it's on your car, stay inside. If you must leave because of fire, jump clear with both feet together and shuffle away without lifting your feet.␀Tornado warning␀Get to the basement or an inside room with no windows on the lowest floor. Cover your head and neck with a mattress or helmets. A car or mobile home is not safe. Get to a sturdy building or a ditch.␀Diarrhea or vomiting␀Use a separate bucket toilet, gloves and hand washing. Rehydration drink: 1 liter clean water, 6 level tsp sugar, 1/2 level tsp salt. Small sips, often. Blood in the stool, high fever, or a baby with no wet diaper for 6+ hours is serious.␀Out of water␀Drain the water heater (power and gas off, tap at the bottom) and the house pipes from the lowest faucet. Then rain, snow, creek or pond water, filtered and treated. Cut back on hard work and salty food. Stay in the shade.␀Pipes about to freeze␀Shut off the main water valve. Open all faucets, starting at the top floor, to drain the lines. Drain the water heater. Pour RV (pink, non-toxic) antifreeze into sink traps and toilet bowls.␀Bitter cold night␀Everyone in one small room, in hats, layered on blankets off the floor. A tent set up indoors keeps a lot of heat in. Eat a fatty snack before bed. Hot water bottles in sleeping bags. Watch kids and elders closely.␀Stranger at the door␀Talk through the door or a window and don't open it. Don't say who's home or what you have. Point them to the neighborhood board, the trade day or the shelter. Keep a partner nearby. Note what they looked like and which way they went.␀Lost child␀Search the house first: closets, under beds, cars, sheds. Note what they're wearing. One person stays home in case they return. Others search outward in pairs along their favorite routes and any water first. Tell neighbors and use the radio net.␀Rumor of danger coming␀Check it with two separate sources (radio, a trusted neighbor). Meanwhile pack the go-bags and fill the car. Decide the line now: "if we hear X from Y, we leave."␀Someone is panicking␀Get close, speak low and slow, and use their name. Give one small job right now: "hold this light," "fill this jug." Breathe with them: in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4. Afterward, give them food, water and a rest.␀Flooding␀Move people, meds, food and dry wood up high. Shut off power at the main only if you can reach it standing on dry ground. Don't walk or drive through moving water. Six inches can knock you down and a foot can float a car.␀Dog or animal bite␀Wash with soap and running water for a full 5 minutes. Let it bleed a little, then cover it. Watch for infection. Bites from bats, raccoons, skunks or foxes, or from an animal acting strangely, carry rabies risk, so get medical help as soon as possible.␀Tick found␀Grab it with fine tweezers right at the skin and pull straight out, slow and steady. Wash the spot. Write down the date. A spreading red rash, fever or aches over the next month may be Lyme disease.␀Chimney fire␀Signs: a loud roaring or rumbling, dense dark smoke, sparks or flames from the top, a stovepipe glowing or ticking. Close the stove door and air intake to starve it, get everyone out, and call for help if possible. From outside, wet the roof around the chimney with a hose if it's safe. Don't use the stove again until the chimney is inspected.␀Smoke pouring into the room␀Open the stove air intake and damper fully and crack a window near the stove. A cold flue drafts badly, so warm it with burning newspaper. Check the chimney cap for snow, ice or a nest. If smoke keeps coming in, let the fire die, ventilate, and watch the CO alarm.␀Nuclear flash or blast␀Drop face down, cover your head and skin, don't look at the light. When the blast passes, get inside the nearest sturdy building fast, to the basement or center. Stay inside at least 24 hours and listen to the radio. If you were outside, remove outer clothing and wash with soap and shampoo, no conditioner.␀Strange smell or many people sick at once␀Possible chemical release. Move upwind and uphill, or get inside. Close windows, shut off fans and HVAC, and go to an upstairs inner room. Seal gaps with plastic, tape or wet towels. If exposed: strip, wash with soap and water, flush eyes 10-15 minutes, and get medical help.␀Drone overhead␀Get under solid cover now: a building, concrete overhang or basement, or dense trees. Caught in the open: lie flat and still in a low spot. Stay away from windows. Never touch a downed drone. Report it.␀␀ù#<ù#</ù#<eù#<ù#<S$<l$<¼$<ù#</
